var e={"gastro-hepatology-acid-peptic-gerd":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Dyspepsia** is epigastric pain, burning, fullness or early satiety for **4 weeks or more**.`,`Only about 1 in 4 dyspeptic patients has an ulcer; most have functional dyspepsia.`,`A **peptic ulcer** is a mucosal break of **5 mm or more** through the muscularis mucosae.`,`**GERD** is reflux causing **troublesome symptoms on 2 or more days a week** or oesophagitis.`,`Most Indian patients have **non-erosive reflux disease**, so a normal endoscopy does not exclude GERD.`,`Oesophagitis is graded **Los Angeles A-D**; under **Lyon 2.0**, LA B-D is conclusive GERD.`,`**Rome IV functional dyspepsia** needs 3 months of symptoms, onset 6 months earlier, and normal endoscopy.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**H. pylori** causes about 90% of duodenal and 70% of gastric ulcers.`,`**NSAIDs and low-dose aspirin** are the other main cause, even enteric-coated or injected.`,`Steroids, SSRIs and anticoagulants added to an NSAID multiply the bleeding risk.`,`Smoking and alcohol impair healing; smoking doubles ulcer relapse.`,`GERD risk rises with **obesity, hiatus hernia** and late heavy meals.`,`Hidden NSAIDs or steroids in **ayurvedic pain powders** are common in India.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Acid comes from the parietal cell **proton pump (H+/K+ ATPase)**, the target of every PPI.`,`Antral H. pylori gastritis lowers somatostatin, so gastrin and acid rise.`,`Excess acid causes duodenal gastric metaplasia, which H. pylori colonises, giving **duodenal ulcer**.`,`Corpus gastritis causes atrophy and intestinal metaplasia: the **Correa cascade** to gastric cancer.`,`NSAIDs block **COX-1**, removing protective prostaglandins E2 and I2.`,`GERD is driven mainly by **transient LOS relaxations (TLESRs)** after large or fatty meals.`,`Hiatus hernia creates an acid pocket that refluxes on lying down.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Duodenal ulcer pain wakes the patient at **2 am and is relieved by food**.`,`Gastric ulcer pain is worsened by food, so the patient eats less and loses weight.`,`Ask about heartburn and regurgitation, how many days a week, and worse on lying.`,`Ask every patient the **alarm questions**: dysphagia, weight loss, vomiting, bleeding, anaemia.`,`**Drug history is the highest-yield question**, including over-the-counter and ayurvedic powders.`,`Ask about past H. pylori treatment and whether cure was tested.`,`Epigastric pain with sweating in a diabetic or smoker needs an **ECG** first.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examination is **usually normal**; any positive finding suggests a complication.`,`Look for **pallor and koilonychia**, and record weight at every visit.`,`Check pulse and postural blood pressure for an occult bleed.`,`Feel for **Virchow node** and an epigastric mass, which suggest gastric cancer.`,`A **succussion splash** means gastric outlet obstruction.`,`Board-like rigidity with absent liver dullness means **perforation**.`,`Do a per-rectal examination for melaena when there is anaemia or dark stools.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Urea breath test or stool antigen** diagnoses H. pylori and proves cure.`,`Stop PPIs **2 weeks** and antibiotics or bismuth **4 weeks** before the test.`,`**Serology is not used in India**, as it cannot separate past from active infection.`,`Endoscopy for any alarm feature, or new dyspepsia from **age 40-45** in India.`,`**Biopsy every gastric ulcer** and re-scope it at 8-12 weeks.`,`Haemoglobin and ferritin are the only routine blood tests.`,`pH-impedance testing is only for refractory symptoms or before anti-reflux surgery.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Weight loss is the most effective lifestyle measure for GERD.`,`Raise the **head end of the bed 15-20 cm on blocks**; no meal within 3 hours of lying.`,`Stop or replace the NSAID; use paracetamol or a topical NSAID.`,`Give the **PPI 30-60 minutes before breakfast**, never after dinner.`,`PPI course: **duodenal ulcer 4 weeks**, gastric ulcer and GERD **8 weeks**.`,`H. pylori first line in India is **bismuth quadruple for 14 days**.`,`Warn that bismuth turns stools black, which is not blood.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Use **twice-daily PPI** for LA C-D oesophagitis, Barrett or extra-oesophageal reflux.`,`Vonoprazan 20 mg BD with amoxicillin 1 g TDS for 14 days is an alternative first line.`,`After failed bismuth quadruple, give **levofloxacin triple for 14 days**.`,`Functional dyspepsia resistant to PPI or prokinetic may respond to **amitriptyline 10-25 mg** at night.`,`Pregnancy: **alginate first**, then omeprazole; never misoprostol or tetracycline.`,`Children: omeprazole **10 mg (10-19 kg) or 20 mg (20 kg or more)** once daily.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Bleeding** is the commonest complication; a posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery.`,`Perforation shows free gas under the diaphragm on erect chest X-ray in about 75%.`,`Gastric outlet obstruction causes **hypochloraemic hypokalaemic alkalosis** with paradoxical aciduria.`,`Long-term reflux leads to stricture or **Barrett oesophagus**, the precursor of adenocarcinoma.`,`Long-term PPI is linked to low magnesium, low B12, C. difficile and fractures.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **4-8 weeks** and step down rather than repeat the prescription.`,`Test of cure **4 weeks after antibiotics and 2 weeks off PPI** in every treated patient.`,`Non-dysplastic Barrett: survey every **5 years if under 3 cm**, 3 years if 3 cm or more.`,`Refer after **two eradication failures** for culture-guided therapy, or after 8 weeks of failed twice-daily PPI.`,`After an ulcer bleed, restart needed aspirin **within 3-5 days** with a PPI.`,`**Gastroprotection**: PPI with an NSAID if age 65 or over, past ulcer, or aspirin, anticoagulant, steroid or SSRI co-therapy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for acid peptic disease, GERD and H. pylori`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Omeprazole / pantoprazole (PPI)`,`Omeprazole 20 mg or pantoprazole 40 mg oral OD, before breakfast`,`DU 4 weeks; GU and GERD 8 weeks`,`Low Mg and B12, C. difficile; pantoprazole with clopidogrel`],[`Vonoprazan (P-CAB)`,`20 mg oral OD, with or without food`,`4-8 weeks; 10 mg maintenance`,`Diarrhoea, hypergastrinaemia`],[`Famotidine (H2 blocker)`,`20 mg BD or 40 mg at bedtime`,`On demand or short course`,`Tachyphylaxis in 2 weeks; halve if CrCl under 50`],[`Sodium alginate-antacid`,`10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime`,`As needed`,`Sodium load; first line in pregnancy`],[`Sucralfate`,`1 g QID, 1 hour before meals`,`4-8 weeks`,`Constipation, aluminium in CKD; space other drugs 2 h`],[`Misoprostol`,`200 microgram two to four times daily with food`,`While on NSAID`,`Diarrhoea; abortifacient, never in pregnancy`],[`Domperidone`,`10 mg TDS before meals, maximum 30 mg/day`,`Up to 1 week`,`QT prolongation; avoid with clarithromycin, azoles`],[`Bismuth quadruple (first line)`,`PPI BD, bismuth 120 mg QID, tetracycline 500 mg QID, metronidazole 400-500 mg TDS`,`14 days`,`Black stools; no alcohol; no tetracycline in pregnancy`],[`Levofloxacin triple (second line)`,`Levofloxacin 500 mg OD, amoxicillin 1 g BD, PPI BD`,`14 days`,`Tendinopathy, QT prolongation; avoid if prior quinolone`]]},{heading:`Los Angeles grading of reflux oesophagitis`,columns:[`Grade`,`Endoscopic finding`,`What to do`],rows:[[`LA A`,`Breaks 5 mm or less, not continuous across folds`,`Borderline GERD; PPI 8 weeks, step down`],[`LA B`,`Breaks over 5 mm, not continuous across folds`,`Conclusive GERD; PPI 8 weeks`],[`LA C`,`Continuous across folds, under 75% of circumference`,`Twice-daily PPI; re-scope for Barrett`],[`LA D`,`75% or more of circumference`,`Twice-daily PPI, long-term PPI or surgery`]]},{heading:`GERD vs functional dyspepsia vs cardiac pain`,columns:[`Feature`,`GERD`,`Functional dyspepsia`,`Cardiac pain`],rows:[[`Site`,`Retrosternal burning rising up`,`Epigastric pain or fullness`,`Epigastric or retrosternal pressure`],[`Trigger`,`Large meals, bending, lying`,`Meals or fasting`,`Exertion, stress`],[`Associated`,`Regurgitation, cough, hoarseness`,`Early satiety, bloating`,`Sweating, breathlessness`],[`Key test`,`PPI trial; endoscopy LA grade`,`Normal endoscopy`,`ECG, troponin`]]}],remember:[`A PPI works only **30-60 minutes before food**; after dinner is the commonest 'PPI failure'.`,`**14-day bismuth quadruple** is first line in India; 7-day clarithromycin triple is obsolete.`,`Off PPI 2 weeks and antibiotics 4 weeks before a breath or stool test.`,`Every gastric ulcer is biopsied and re-scoped; a duodenal ulcer is not.`,`Black stools on bismuth are bismuth sulphide, not melaena.`,`Ranitidine is withdrawn (NDMA); write **famotidine**.`,`Pantoprazole with clopidogrel.`,`Review every PPI at 8 weeks; no indefinite PPI without an indication.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - acid peptic disease, GERD and H. pylori`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Dyspepsia** - epigastric pain/burning, fullness or early satiety for **4 weeks or more**; **peptic ulcer** - mucosal break **5 mm or more through muscularis mucosae**; **GERD** - reflux causing **troublesome symptoms (2 or more days a week) or oesophagitis**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Los Angeles A-D** for oesophagitis (**Lyon 2.0**: LA B-D, long-segment Barrett or stricture = conclusive GERD; LA A borderline); **Rome IV functional dyspepsia** - epigastric pain syndrome vs postprandial distress syndrome.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**H. pylori (90% DU, 70% GU) and NSAIDs/low-dose aspirin**; steroids, SSRIs, anticoagulants add bleed risk; smoking, alcohol; GERD - **obesity, hiatus hernia**, late heavy meals; Indian H. pylori seroprevalence **60-80%**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Antral H. pylori -> **low somatostatin -> high gastrin and acid** -> duodenal gastric metaplasia -> **DU**; corpus gastritis -> atrophy -> **Correa cascade** -> GU, cancer, MALToma; NSAID -> **COX-1 block -> loss of PGE2/PGI2**; GERD -> **TLESRs** + acid pocket.`],[`Key history`,`Pain timing (DU **2 am, relieved by food**; GU worse with food), heartburn/regurgitation frequency, **alarm features (dysphagia, weight loss, vomiting, bleeding, anaemia)**, NSAIDs incl. ayurvedic powders, past eradication, family history of gastric cancer, cardiac symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Usually normal; **pallor, weight, postural drop**, Virchow node, epigastric mass, **succussion splash** (outlet obstruction), rigidity with absent liver dullness (perforation), PR for melaena.`],[`Investigations`,`**UBT or stool antigen** (off PPI **2 weeks**, antibiotics/bismuth **4 weeks**); **no serology**; Hb +/- ferritin; **endoscopy for alarm features or new dyspepsia at 40-45+ (India)**; biopsy every GU; pH-impedance only for refractory/pre-surgery.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`GERD - typical symptoms 2+ days/week or **LA B-D** on endoscopy; functional dyspepsia - **3 months symptoms, onset 6 months earlier, normal endoscopy**; ulcer - endoscopic mucosal break 5 mm or more.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**PPI 30-60 min before breakfast** (omeprazole 20, pantoprazole 40, esomeprazole 40 mg): DU 4 weeks, GU/GERD 8 weeks; **H. pylori - bismuth quadruple 14 days**: PPI BD + bismuth 120 mg QID + **tetracycline 500 mg QID + metronidazole 400-500 mg TDS**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Twice-daily PPI for LA C-D, Barrett, extra-oesophageal; **vonoprazan 20 mg** (erosive); **vonoprazan 20 mg BD + amoxicillin 1 g TDS 14 days** (ACG 2024); failed bismuth -> **levofloxacin 500 mg OD + amoxicillin 1 g BD + PPI BD 14 days**; 2 failures -> culture-guided.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Raise **head end of bed 15-20 cm on blocks**, left lateral sleep, no meal within 3 h of lying; endoscopic dual therapy for bleeding; dilatation for outlet obstruction; fundoplication for PPI-uncontrolled regurgitation.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy - alginate first**, then PPI (omeprazole); **no misoprostol or tetracycline**; child omeprazole **10 mg (10-19 kg), 20 mg (20 kg+)**; clopidogrel - **pantoprazole**; elderly on NSAID - PPI cover; famotidine halve dose if CrCl under 50.`],[`Complications`,`**Bleeding** (posterior DU -> gastroduodenal artery), **perforation** (free gas in 75%), **gastric outlet obstruction** (hypochloraemic hypokalaemic alkalosis, paradoxical aciduria), stricture, **Barrett -> adenocarcinoma**, long-term PPI harms (low Mg, B12, C. difficile, fractures).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Haematemesis/melaena, progressive dysphagia, weight loss, persistent vomiting, mass, **iron deficiency**, new dyspepsia at 40-45+, exertional epigastric pain (**do ECG**); refer after 2 eradication failures or 8 weeks of BD PPI failure.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **4-8 weeks** and step down; **test of cure 4 weeks after antibiotics, 2 weeks off PPI**; re-scope GU at **8-12 weeks** and LA C-D after healing; Barrett surveillance **5 years (under 3 cm) / 3 years (3 cm+)**; review every PPI at 8 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`PPI gastroprotection with NSAIDs if **age 65+, past ulcer, aspirin/anticoagulant/steroid/SSRI**; test-and-treat before long-term aspirin; screen and eradicate in first-degree relatives of gastric cancer; safe water and hygiene.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed in this pass - Maastricht VI 2022, ACG H. pylori 2024, ACG GERD 2022 and Lyon 2.0 remain current; **7-day clarithromycin triple is obsolete**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`PPI before food, not after dinner; **black stools on bismuth are not melaena**; ranitidine withdrawn (NDMA) - write famotidine; serology useless in India; every GU biopsied; pantoprazole with clopidogrel.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dyspepsia in the OPD - the exam algorithm`,caption:`Alarm features and age decide endoscopy; otherwise test and treat.`,steps:[{label:`Dyspepsia 4 weeks or more`,detail:`Check drugs (NSAID, aspirin), ECG if cardiac risk`},{label:`Alarm feature or age 40-45+?`,detail:`Dysphagia, weight loss, bleeding, anaemia, vomiting, mass`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - urgent endoscopy with biopsy`,detail:`Biopsy every gastric ulcer; RUT plus histology`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No - test and treat H. pylori`,detail:`UBT or stool antigen; off PPI 2 weeks, antibiotics 4 weeks`},{label:`Positive - bismuth quadruple 14 days`,detail:`PPI BD + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole`},{label:`Negative - PPI 4-8 weeks`,detail:`Prokinetic if postprandial distress`},{label:`Confirm cure and step down`,detail:`Test of cure at 4 weeks; PPI review at 8 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`H. pylori eradication ladder (India)`,caption:`Always 14 days; always confirm cure.`,steps:[{label:`First line: bismuth quadruple`,detail:`PPI BD, bismuth 120 mg QID, tetracycline 500 mg QID, metronidazole 400-500 mg TDS x 14 d`},{label:`Alternative first line`,detail:`Vonoprazan 20 mg BD + amoxicillin 1 g TDS x 14 d; or concomitant quadruple if no bismuth`},{label:`Second line: levofloxacin triple`,detail:`Levofloxacin 500 mg OD + amoxicillin 1 g BD + PPI BD x 14 d`},{label:`Two failures: culture-guided`,detail:`Endoscopy, culture or molecular resistance testing`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Proven cure`,detail:`UBT/stool antigen 4 weeks after therapy, 2 weeks off PPI`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`GERD vs functional dyspepsia vs cardiac pain`,columns:[`Feature`,`GERD`,`Functional dyspepsia`,`Cardiac (inferior MI/angina)`],rows:[[`Site`,`Retrosternal burning rising up`,`Epigastric pain or fullness`,`Epigastric or retrosternal pressure`],[`Trigger`,`Large meals, bending, lying`,`Meals (PDS), fasting (EPS)`,`Exertion, stress`],[`Associated`,`Regurgitation, cough, hoarseness`,`Early satiety, bloating`,`Sweating, breathlessness`],[`Key test`,`PPI trial; endoscopy LA grade`,`Normal endoscopy, H. pylori negative`,`ECG, troponin`],[`Treatment`,`Lifestyle + PPI 8 weeks`,`PPI or prokinetic, amitriptyline`,`Emergency referral`]]}]},"gastro-hepatology-acute-diarrhoea":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute diarrhoea** is 3 or more loose or watery stools in 24 hours, lasting **under 14 days**.`,`**Persistent diarrhoea** lasts 14 days or more; **chronic diarrhoea** lasts 4 weeks or more.`,`**Dysentery** is diarrhoea with **visible blood**, and it is the syndrome that needs an antibiotic.`,`The pasty stool of a breastfed baby is not diarrhoea.`,`People die of **dehydration, not of the organism**, so rehydration always comes first.`,`Diarrhoea is still a leading cause of **under-five deaths in India**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify into acute watery, dysentery, persistent, or diarrhoea with severe malnutrition.`,`Grade dehydration by **IMNCI: no, some or severe**, linked to **Plans A, B and C**.`,`Two or more signs in a column place the child in that grade.`,`By weight loss: **under 5% mild, 5-10% moderate, over 10% severe**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Cholera toxin** keeps adenylate cyclase switched on, so cAMP rises inside the enterocyte.`,`The **CFTR chloride channel** pumps chloride into the lumen and water follows: painless rice-water stool.`,`The **SGLT1 sodium-glucose co-transporter** is untouched by toxins, which is **why ORS works**.`,`Rotavirus kills villous tip cells, causing temporary lactase deficiency.`,`**Shigella** invades the colon and causes bloody stool; its Shiga toxin can cause **HUS**.`,`Stool loses sodium, potassium and bicarbonate, giving **normal anion gap acidosis** and hypokalaemia.`,`Zinc is lost in stool; replacing it shortens the episode by about 25%.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask duration, number and volume of stools, and **blood or mucus** in stool.`,`Ask about vomiting, ability to drink, and **when urine was last passed**.`,`Vomiting within **1-6 hours** of a shared meal suggests a preformed toxin.`,`Ask about **other cases nearby and the water source**; a cluster means an outbreak.`,`Ask about antibiotics in the last 8 weeks, which points to C. difficile.`,`In a child, ask about breastfeeding, rotavirus and measles vaccines, and check the growth card.`,`Severe pain, bilious vomiting or blood without diarrhoea suggests a surgical cause such as intussusception.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**General condition** is the most important sign: alert, restless, or lethargic.`,`Look for **sunken eyes** and watch whether the child drinks normally, eagerly, or poorly.`,`**Skin pinch** going back very slowly (over 2 seconds) means severe dehydration.`,`Record pulse, capillary refill, blood pressure, breathing rate and **weight**.`,`Deep rapid breathing means acidosis; distension with absent bowel sounds suggests ileus.`,`Look for malnutrition: **MUAC under 11.5 cm** or bilateral pitting oedema means SAM.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Most acute watery diarrhoea needs no test.**`,`Stool microscopy for dysentery: **trophozoites with ingested red cells** prove amoebiasis.`,`Hanging-drop microscopy and stool culture in suspected cholera or an outbreak.`,`Sodium, potassium, creatinine and glucose in severe dehydration or convulsions.`,`C. difficile toxin or PCR only after recent antibiotics or hospital stay.`,`After bloody diarrhoea with pallor or oliguria, check blood count, smear and creatinine for HUS.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Plan A**: ORS after each loose stool, 50-100 mL under 2 years, 100-200 mL at 2-10 years.`,`**Plan B**: ORS **75 mL/kg over 4 hours**, then reassess.`,`**Plan C**: IV **Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg** on the age schedule, then ORS once drinking.`,`**Zinc for 14 days** in every child: 10 mg at 2-6 months, 20 mg over 6 months.`,`**Continue feeding and breastfeeding**, plus one extra meal a day for 2 weeks.`,`One ORS sachet in exactly **1 litre of clean water**; discard after 24 hours.`,`**No antibiotics for watery diarrhoea.**`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Child dysentery: **azithromycin 10 mg/kg once daily for 3 days** (IAP preferred).`,`Adult dysentery: **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 3 days**.`,`Cholera after rehydration: adult **doxycycline 300 mg single dose**; child or pregnancy azithromycin 20 mg/kg single.`,`Amoebiasis: metronidazole, then a **luminal amoebicide** such as diloxanide furoate to clear cysts.`,`Giardiasis: **tinidazole 2 g single dose** with food.`,`A single dose of **ondansetron** may be given when vomiting stops ORS in a child over 6 months.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Severe acute malnutrition**: use **ReSoMal**, not standard Plan B, and avoid rapid IV fluids.`,`Pregnancy: azithromycin is preferred; avoid tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones.`,`Adults on sick days: pause **metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, diuretics and NSAIDs**.`,`**Loperamide is never given** to children under 12, in dysentery or in suspected C. difficile.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Lethargy, weak pulse or very slow skin pinch means **severe dehydration**: start Ringer lactate now.`,`Check **blood glucose** in every drowsy or convulsing child.`,`Convulsions during rehydration suggest a sodium problem; correct hypernatraemia slowly.`,`Pallor, oliguria and petechiae after bloody diarrhoea suggest **HUS**.`,`Distension with absent bowel sounds during dysentery means **toxic megacolon**.`,`Review dysentery at **2 days** and Plan A children at **5 days** if diarrhoea continues.`,`Notify cholera and diarrhoea clusters through **IDSP-IHIP within 24 hours**; chlorinate the water source.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and fluids for acute diarrhoea`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Low-osmolarity ORS (245 mOsm/L)`,`Plan A per stool; Plan B 75 mL/kg oral`,`Plan B over 4 hours`,`Vomiting if too fast; never make stronger`],[`Zinc dispersible`,`10 mg (2-6 months), 20 mg (over 6 months) OD`,`14 days`,`Vomiting; repeat if vomited within 30 min`],[`Ringer lactate`,`100 mL/kg IV by age schedule`,`3 hours (1 year+), 6 hours (infant)`,`Fluid overload in SAM and heart failure`],[`Azithromycin`,`Child 10 mg/kg OD; adult 500 mg OD`,`3 days (dysentery)`,`Nausea, QT prolongation; safe in pregnancy`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`Adult 500 mg BD; child 15 mg/kg BD`,`3 days`,`High Shigella resistance; tendinopathy`],[`Doxycycline`,`300 mg oral, adult cholera`,`Single dose`,`Not in pregnancy or under 8 years`],[`Metronidazole / tinidazole`,`Metronidazole 400-800 mg TDS; tinidazole 2 g`,`5-10 days; tinidazole single for giardia`,`Metallic taste; no alcohol for 48-72 h`],[`Diloxanide furoate`,`500 mg TDS (child 20 mg/kg/day)`,`10 days`,`Flatulence; give after tissue amoebicide`],[`Ondansetron`,`Single oral dose 0.15 mg/kg`,`Once`,`QT prolongation; do not repeat`],[`Oral vancomycin / fidaxomicin`,`Vancomycin 125 mg QID or fidaxomicin 200 mg BD`,`10 days`,`C. difficile only; oral route`]]},{heading:`IMNCI dehydration grading and plan`,columns:[`Sign`,`No dehydration (Plan A)`,`Some dehydration (Plan B)`,`Severe dehydration (Plan C)`],rows:[[`General condition`,`Well, alert`,`Restless, irritable`,`Lethargic or unconscious`],[`Eyes`,`Normal`,`Sunken`,`Sunken`],[`Drinking`,`Drinks normally`,`Thirsty, drinks eagerly`,`Drinks poorly or not at all`],[`Skin pinch`,`Goes back immediately`,`Goes back slowly`,`Very slowly, over 2 seconds`],[`Fluid`,`ORS after each stool at home`,`ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours`,`Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg IV`]]},{heading:`Plan C - Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg`,columns:[`Age`,`First 30 mL/kg`,`Then 70 mL/kg`],rows:[[`Under 12 months`,`Over 1 hour`,`Over 5 hours`],[`12 months and over, adults`,`Over 30 minutes`,`Over 2.5 hours`],[`Radial pulse still weak`,`Repeat first bolus once`,`Then continue schedule`]]}],remember:[`Low-osmolarity ORS: **sodium 75, glucose 75, total 245 mOsm/L**.`,`Zinc **10 mg under 6 months, 20 mg over 6 months, for 14 days**.`,`Plan B is **75 mL/kg in 4 hours**; Plan C is **Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg**.`,`Antibiotics only for dysentery, cholera, amoebiasis, giardiasis and systemic illness.`,`Trophozoites with ingested red cells, not cysts, prove invasive amoebiasis.`,`Never stop breastfeeding or food during diarrhoea.`,`A cluster in one colony is a water problem: chlorinate, sample, notify.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - acute diarrhoea and dysentery`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**3 or more loose/watery stools in 24 h, under 14 days**; **persistent** 14 days or more; **chronic** 4 weeks or more; **dysentery = visible blood** in stool.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IMNCI: no / some / severe dehydration -> Plan A / B / C**; clinical type - acute watery, dysentery, persistent, diarrhoea with SAM; weight loss under 5% mild, 5-10% moderate, over 10% severe.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Rotavirus, norovirus, **ETEC**, V. cholerae, **Shigella**, Campylobacter, Salmonella, **E. histolytica**, Giardia, preformed toxins (S. aureus, B. cereus); unsafe water, no handwashing, malnutrition, no rotavirus/measles vaccine.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cholera toxin -> **Gs-alpha ADP-ribosylation -> cAMP -> CFTR chloride secretion** -> rice-water stool; **SGLT1 (2 Na + 1 glucose) intact -> ORS works**; Shigella invasion -> ulcers, Shiga toxin -> **HUS**; stool loss -> ECF depletion, **NAGMA, hypokalaemia**.`],[`Key history`,`Stool number, volume, **blood**, vomiting, **urine output (none 6-8 h)**, fever, food 1-6 h earlier (toxin), cluster and water source, drugs (antibiotics - C. difficile), feeding, vaccines, sick-day drugs in adults.`],[`Key examination`,`**General condition, eyes, thirst, skin pinch (over 2 s = very slow)**, pulse, CRT, Kussmaul breathing, weight, **MUAC under 11.5 cm / oedema (SAM)**, abdomen for ileus or surgical cause.`],[`Investigations`,`**None for most watery diarrhoea**; stool microscopy for dysentery (**trophozoites with ingested RBCs**), hanging drop/culture for cholera; electrolytes, creatinine, glucose if severe; CBC, smear, creatinine if HUS suspected.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Some dehydration = **2 or more of**: restless, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly, skin pinch slow; severe = **2 or more of**: lethargic, sunken eyes, drinks poorly, pinch very slow.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Low-osmolarity ORS 245 mOsm/L**: Plan A after each stool (**50-100 mL under 2 y, 100-200 mL 2-10 y**); Plan B **75 mL/kg over 4 h**; **zinc 10 mg (2-6 months) / 20 mg (over 6 months) for 14 days**; continue feeding.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Plan C **RL 100 mL/kg**: under 12 m **30 mL/kg in 1 h then 70 in 5 h**; older **30 in 30 min then 70 in 2.5 h**; NG 20 mL/kg/h if no IV; dysentery **azithromycin 10 mg/kg x 3 d** (or cipro 15 mg/kg BD x 3 d, IMNCI); cholera **doxycycline 300 mg single / azithromycin 20 mg/kg**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`ORS: **1 sachet in 1 L clean water, discard after 24 h**, cup and spoon; intraosseous line if IV fails in 90 s; cholera cot to measure output; **chlorinate to residual 0.5 mg/L** in outbreaks.`],[`Special groups`,`**SAM - ReSoMal 5 mL/kg every 30 min x 2 h, then 5-10 mL/kg/h**; pregnancy - azithromycin, avoid tetracyclines and quinolones; adults - **hold metformin, SGLT2i, ACEi/ARB, diuretics, NSAIDs**; single-dose ondansetron 0.15 mg/kg in vomiting child over 6 months.`],[`Complications`,`Shock, **AKI**, hypo/hypernatraemia (seizures), **hypokalaemia (ileus)**, NAGMA, hypoglycaemia, **HUS** after STEC/Shigella, toxic megacolon, GBS after Campylobacter, malnutrition.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Lethargy, cannot drink, weak pulse, no urine 6-8 h, convulsions, **blood in stool under 2 months**, SAM, suspected HUS, surgical abdomen, persistent diarrhoea, adult over 50 with weight loss or nocturnal stools.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Plan A review in **5 days**, dysentery in **2 days** (change antibiotic, think amoebiasis); reassess every 1-2 h in Plan B and 15-30 min in Plan C; zinc adherence 14 days; extra meal a day for 2 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**STOP Diarrhoea Campaign** (ORS-zinc co-pack, ORS-zinc corners), **rotavirus vaccine 6, 10, 14 weeks (UIP)**, measles-rubella, vitamin A, exclusive breastfeeding, WASH; **notify clusters via IDSP-IHIP**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed in this pass - WHO 2024 diarrhoea guideline, IMNCI and STOP Diarrhoea remain current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Death is from dehydration, not the bug; **ORS Na 75, Cl 65, glucose 75, K 20, citrate 10**; no antibiotics for watery diarrhoea; **loperamide never under 12 or in dysentery**; follow metronidazole with a luminal amoebicide.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Diarrhoea: assess, classify, plan`,caption:`IMNCI sequence written as an answer.`,steps:[{label:`Assess general condition, eyes, thirst, skin pinch`,detail:`Plus weight, MUAC, blood in stool, duration`},{label:`2+ severe signs?`,detail:`Lethargic, drinks poorly, pinch over 2 s`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Plan C - IV Ringer lactate 100 mL/kg`,detail:`Under 12 m: 30 in 1 h, 70 in 5 h; older 30 in 30 min, 70 in 2.5 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`2+ some signs - Plan B`,detail:`ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 h, then reassess`},{label:`No dehydration - Plan A at home`,detail:`ORS per stool, zinc 14 days, feed, danger signs`},{label:`Blood, cholera or SAM?`,detail:`Azithromycin / doxycycline / ReSoMal`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Rehydrated, feeding, zinc completed`,detail:`Review 5 days (2 days for dysentery)`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Watery diarrhoea vs cholera vs bacillary vs amoebic dysentery`,columns:[`Feature`,`Viral/ETEC watery`,`Cholera`,`Shigella`,`Amoebic`],rows:[[`Stool`,`Watery`,`Rice-water, huge volume`,`Small, blood and pus`,`Bloody mucoid, offensive`],[`Fever`,`Low or none`,`None`,`High`,`Little`],[`Key test`,`None`,`Hanging drop, culture`,`Pus cells, culture`,`Trophozoites with RBCs`],[`Drug`,`ORS + zinc only`,`Doxycycline 300 mg single`,`Azithromycin 3 days`,`Metronidazole then diloxanide`],[`Danger`,`Dehydration`,`Rapid shock`,`HUS, toxic megacolon`,`Liver abscess`]]}]},"gastro-hepatology-jaundice":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Jaundice** is yellow sclerae and skin from raised serum bilirubin.`,`Normal bilirubin is under 1.2 mg/dL; jaundice is **visible at about 2.5-3 mg/dL**.`,`**Scleral icterus** separates true jaundice from carotenaemia, which spares the sclerae.`,`**Acute liver failure** is INR 1.5 or more plus any encephalopathy, without prior liver disease.`,`Common Indian causes: **hepatitis E and A**, alcohol, ATT and herbal drugs, malaria, leptospirosis, gallstones.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Put every patient in one of **three boxes**: prehepatic, hepatocellular or cholestatic.`,`Direct bilirubin over 15-20% of total always means liver or biliary disease.`,`**R factor** is (ALT/ULN) divided by (ALP/ULN).`,`R factor **5 or more is hepatocellular**, 2 or less cholestatic, 2-5 mixed.`,`**DILI** is ALT 5x ULN or more, ALP 2x ULN or more, or ALT 3x ULN with bilirubin over 2x ULN.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`About **80% of bilirubin** comes from old red cells broken down in macrophages.`,`Unconjugated bilirubin is bound to albumin, so it **never appears in urine**.`,`The liver conjugates it with **UGT1A1**, which is reduced in Gilbert syndrome.`,`Excretion into bile by **MRP2** is the rate-limiting step and fails first in liver injury.`,`Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble, so it darkens the urine.`,`Complete obstruction gives **pale clay stools** and no urinary urobilinogen.`,`No bile in the gut means poor fat absorption and **vitamin K deficiency**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **urine and stool colour first**; it sorts the patient into a box.`,`A prodrome with distaste for food suggests acute viral hepatitis.`,`**Painless deepening jaundice with weight loss** suggests malignant obstruction.`,`Fever with rigors, right upper quadrant pain and jaundice is **Charcot triad**: cholangitis.`,`Take a **6-month drug history**, including ATT, paracetamol, amoxicillin-clavulanate and antiepileptics.`,`Ask by name about **ayurvedic medicines, liver tonics, giloy and gym supplements**.`,`Ask about floodwater exposure, travel, and pregnancy and its trimester.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for icterus in **natural daylight**; tube light masks it.`,`Scratch marks suggest cholestasis; pallor suggests haemolysis or malignancy.`,`Look for **stigmata of chronic liver disease** such as spider naevi and palmar erythema.`,`**Asterixis**, fetor or confusion means encephalopathy and admission.`,`A palpable non-tender gallbladder suggests malignant distal obstruction (**Courvoisier law**).`,`A shrinking liver span in acute hepatitis signals massive necrosis.`,`Look for calf tenderness and conjunctival suffusion (leptospirosis) and an eschar (scrub typhus).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`First line: bilirubin, **ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, albumin and INR**, with blood count and reticulocytes.`,`**Ultrasound is the single most useful next test**; it shows whether the ducts are dilated.`,`**Dilated ducts** lead to MRCP or endoscopic ultrasound; ERCP is kept for therapy.`,`Non-dilated ducts lead to viral serology and the drug history.`,`Viral markers: **IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HEV, HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc and anti-HCV**.`,`Febrile jaundice: malaria test, dengue, **leptospira IgM**, scrub typhus IgM and blood cultures.`,`**Gilbert syndrome**: unconjugated bilirubin under 4-5 mg/dL, normal enzymes, no haemolysis.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat the cause, not the colour**; liver tonics and glucose drips have no proven benefit.`,`Give a **normal balanced diet** with adequate protein; a fat-free diet only wastes muscle.`,`Stop alcohol and **every non-essential and herbal medicine**.`,`Avoid sedatives and NSAIDs; use **paracetamol up to 2-3 g a day**.`,`Use **ondansetron** for vomiting rather than sedating antiemetics.`,`Acute viral hepatitis settles in **4-8 weeks**; teach the danger signs.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Cholestatic itch: **cholestyramine** first, then rifampicin, naltrexone and sertraline.`,`Prolonged INR in cholestasis: **vitamin K 10 mg IV or SC daily for 3 days**.`,`If INR corrects with vitamin K it was cholestasis; if not, **synthesis has failed**.`,`Paracetamol overdose: start **IV N-acetylcysteine** on suspicion, without waiting for levels.`,`Cholangitis: blood cultures, IV piperacillin-tazobactam, and **ERCP drainage**.`,`Leptospirosis with jaundice: IV penicillin G or ceftriaxone for 7 days.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**ATT hepatitis**: stop H, R and Z if ALT over 5x ULN, or over 3x ULN with symptoms.`,`Reintroduce at ALT under 2x ULN: **rifampicin first**, isoniazid after 3-7 days, usually no pyrazinamide.`,`Severe TB during ATT hepatitis needs a holding regimen of levofloxacin, ethambutol and an injectable.`,`Neonatal jaundice **within 24 hours of birth** is always pathological.`,`Term baby jaundiced **beyond 14 days with pale stools** has biliary atresia until proved otherwise.`,`Third-trimester itch with bile acids 19 micromol/L or more is **intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Confusion, sleep reversal or asterixis means **hepatic encephalopathy**: admit today.`,`**A falling ALT with rising INR and bilirubin** is massive necrosis, not recovery.`,`In acute liver failure, check glucose every 2-4 hours and refer to a transplant centre the same day.`,`Do not give FFP just to correct INR in liver failure without bleeding.`,`Refer urgently for dilated ducts, a palpable gallbladder, or a baby needing Kasai before 45-60 days.`,`Notify **clusters of jaundice** through IDSP-IHIP; they suggest water-borne hepatitis A or E.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in the jaundiced patient`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cholestyramine`,`4 g before and after breakfast; max 16 g/day`,`While itching`,`Binds other drugs: 1 h before or 4-6 h after`],[`Rifampicin (for itch)`,`150 mg daily, up to 300 mg/day`,`While itching`,`Hepatitis; LFT at 2 and 6 weeks`],[`Naltrexone`,`12.5 mg daily, titrated to 50 mg`,`While itching`,`Opioid-withdrawal reaction; not in decompensation`],[`Ursodeoxycholic acid`,`13-15 mg/kg/day (PBC); 500 mg BD (pregnancy cholestasis)`,`PBC lifelong`,`Diarrhoea, weight gain`],[`Vitamin K (phytomenadione)`,`10 mg slow IV or SC daily`,`3 days`,`Anaphylactoid reaction if IV too fast`],[`N-acetylcysteine IV`,`150 mg/kg over 1 h, 50 over 4 h, 100 over 16 h`,`21 hours`,`Anaphylactoid: pause, antihistamine, restart slower`],[`Paracetamol`,`500 mg-1 g up to TDS; max 2-3 g/day`,`As needed`,`Count combination products`],[`Ondansetron`,`4 mg oral up to TDS; max 8 mg/day if severe`,`As needed`,`QT prolongation, constipation`]]},{heading:`Three types of jaundice`,columns:[`Feature`,`Prehepatic`,`Hepatocellular`,`Cholestatic`],rows:[[`Bilirubin`,`Unconjugated over 80-85%`,`Both fractions raised`,`Mainly conjugated`],[`Urine`,`Normal colour, urobilinogen high`,`Dark, bilirubin present`,`Dark, no urobilinogen`],[`Stool`,`Normal or dark`,`Normal or pale`,`Pale, clay-coloured`],[`Itch`,`Absent`,`Variable`,`Prominent`],[`Enzymes`,`Normal; LDH high, haptoglobin low`,`ALT and AST markedly raised`,`ALP and GGT markedly raised`],[`Causes`,`Haemolysis, malaria, G6PD, Gilbert`,`Viral, alcohol, drugs, leptospirosis`,`Gallstones, pancreatic cancer, PBC`]]},{heading:`Liver test patterns`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Clue`,`Think of`],rows:[[`Hepatocellular`,`R factor 5 or more; ALT may exceed 1000`,`Virus, paracetamol, ischaemia, ATT`],[`Alcohol`,`AST:ALT over 2, both under 300`,`Alcohol-related liver disease`],[`Mixed`,`R factor 2-5`,`DILI, sepsis, infiltration`],[`Cholestatic`,`R factor 2 or less; ALP and GGT high`,`Obstruction, drug cholestasis, PBC`],[`Isolated bilirubin`,`Unconjugated under 4-5 mg/dL, enzymes normal`,`Gilbert syndrome, haemolysis`]]}],remember:[`Ask **urine and stool colour** before ordering any test.`,`Transaminases measure injury; **INR and albumin measure function**.`,`R factor: **5 or more hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic**.`,`ALT over 1000 means virus, drug, toxin or ischaemia.`,`Dilated ducts go to MRCP; non-dilated ducts go back to serology and drugs.`,`ATT restart: **rifampicin first**, then isoniazid, usually leave out pyrazinamide.`,`G6PD assay during acute haemolysis can be falsely normal; repeat in 2-3 months.`]},updated:[`AASLD DILI 2023: causality supported by RUCAM or its evidence-based electronic revision RECAM.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - approach to jaundice and DILI`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Yellow sclerae/skin from bilirubin; normal **under 1.2 mg/dL**, clinically visible at **2.5-3 mg/dL**; **conjugated** if direct over 15-20% of total (or over 1 mg/dL); **ALF = INR 1.5+ with encephalopathy within 26 weeks**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Prehepatic, hepatocellular, cholestatic** (intra/extrahepatic); **R factor = (ALT/ULN)/(ALP/ULN): 5+ hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic, 2-5 mixed**; O'Grady hyperacute / acute / subacute ALF.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India: **hepatitis E and A, alcohol, drugs (ATT, herbal - giloy, ashwagandha), malaria, leptospirosis, gallstones, malignant obstruction**; haemolysis, G6PD, Gilbert; pregnancy (ICP, AFLP, HELLP).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Haem -> biliverdin -> **unconjugated bilirubin (albumin-bound, not in urine)** -> **UGT1A1 conjugation** -> **MRP2 excretion (rate-limiting)** -> gut urobilinogen; obstruction -> **dark urine, pale stool, itch, vitamin K malabsorption**.`],[`Key history`,`**Urine and stool colour first**, prodrome, pain/fever (**Charcot triad**), itch, weight loss, **6-month drug and herbal history**, alcohol (AUDIT-C), faeco-oral vs parenteral risk, monsoon fever exposures, pregnancy, family history.`],[`Key examination`,`Icterus in daylight, scratch marks, **asterixis, fetor**, bruising, CLD stigmata, liver size/shrinking span, **Courvoisier gallbladder**, Murphy sign, splenomegaly, ascites, KF rings, eschar, calf tenderness.`],[`Investigations`,`Bilirubin (total/direct), **ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, albumin, INR**, CBC with smear and retics, urine bilirubin/urobilinogen, **USG for duct dilatation (CBD over 6 mm)**; IgM HAV, IgM HEV, HBsAg + IgM anti-HBc, anti-HCV; febrile panel.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**DILI: ALT 5x ULN, or ALP 2x ULN, or ALT 3x + bilirubin over 2x ULN**; temporal relation 5-90 days, exclusion, dechallenge; **RUCAM or RECAM** for causality; **Hy's law** - hepatocellular injury with jaundice ~10% mortality.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Treat the cause; **stop the drug and all herbals**, normal diet, no alcohol; itch - **cholestyramine 4 g before and after breakfast (max 16 g)**; vitamin K **10 mg slow IV x 3 days** if INR high; paracetamol **max 2-3 g/day**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Itch ladder: **rifampicin 150-300 mg -> naltrexone 12.5->50 mg -> sertraline 75-100 mg**; paracetamol overdose **NAC 150 mg/kg/1 h, 50 mg/kg/4 h, 100 mg/kg/16 h**; NAC in early non-paracetamol ALF; cholangitis **pip-taz 4.5 g + ERCP**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Dilated ducts -> **MRCP/EUS, ERCP for therapy**; urgent biliary drainage in severe cholangitis; neonatal **Kasai before 45-60 days**; avoid FFP to correct INR in ALF.`],[`Special groups`,`**ATT hepatitis**: stop H, R, Z if **ALT over 5x ULN (asymptomatic) or 3x with symptoms/jaundice**; reintroduce at ALT under 2x - **rifampicin first, isoniazid after 3-7 days, usually no pyrazinamide**; pregnancy - **ICP bile acids 19+ micromol/L** (100+ = stillbirth risk); neonate jaundice under 24 h pathological.`],[`Complications`,`**Acute liver failure** (cerebral oedema, hypoglycaemia, sepsis), cholangitis, coagulopathy, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, kernicterus (neonate), ACLF from herbal DILI.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Encephalopathy, INR 1.5+**, rising INR with falling ALT, hypoglycaemia, persistent vomiting, cholangitis, jaundice in pregnancy, painless jaundice with weight loss, pale stools beyond 14 days in a baby.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Bilirubin, ALT, **INR weekly** till falling; enzymes normal by 8-12 weeks, persistence over 6 months -> chronic work-up; ATT LFT if symptoms, 2-4 weekly in high risk; G6PD assay **2-3 months after haemolysis**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Safe water (HAV/HEV), hepatitis B vaccine, **report DILI to PvPI**, NTEP baseline LFT in high risk, notify jaundice clusters via **IDSP-IHIP**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AASLD DILI 2023**: causality by **RUCAM or the electronic revision RECAM**; other thresholds unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Urine and stool colour sort the box; **INR and albumin measure function, ALT measures injury**; AST:ALT over 2 = alcohol; ALT over 1000 = virus, drug, ischaemia; INR corrected by vitamin K = cholestasis.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Jaundice work-up in five moves`,caption:`History box -> LFT pattern -> ultrasound -> targeted tests -> severity.`,steps:[{label:`Urine and stool colour`,detail:`Normal urine = prehepatic; dark urine + pale stool = cholestatic`},{label:`LFT pattern and R factor`,detail:`R 5+ hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ultrasound: ducts dilated?`,detail:`CBD over 6 mm -> MRCP/EUS, ERCP for therapy`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Non-dilated: serology and drug history`,detail:`IgM HAV/HEV, HBsAg, anti-HCV, febrile panel, RUCAM/RECAM`},{label:`INR 1.5+ or encephalopathy?`,detail:`ALF - refer same day to transplant centre`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Treat cause, stop drugs, review weekly`,detail:`INR, bilirubin, ALT until falling`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`ATT-induced hepatitis: stop and reintroduce (NTEP)`,steps:[{label:`ALT over 5x ULN, or 3x with symptoms/jaundice`,detail:`Stop isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide together`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Severe TB: holding regimen`,detail:`Levofloxacin + ethambutol + streptomycin/amikacin`},{label:`ALT under 2x ULN, bilirubin normal`,detail:`Restart rifampicin (with ethambutol)`},{label:`After 3-7 days add isoniazid`,detail:`Check ALT before each addition`},{label:`Usually omit pyrazinamide`,detail:`Extend regimen duration accordingly`,tone:`good`}]}]},"gastro-hepatology-viral-hepatitis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Viral hepatitis** is liver inflammation caused by the hepatotropic viruses A to E.`,`**Chronic hepatitis** means HBsAg or HCV RNA still positive at **6 months**.`,`**Hepatitis A and E** are faeco-oral and never chronic in healthy people.`,`**Hepatitis B, C and D** are blood-borne and can become chronic.`,`India has HBsAg about **1-2%** and anti-HCV about **0.5-1%**.`,`Hepatitis E and A are leading causes of **acute liver failure** in India.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify **by route and chronicity**: enteric (A, E) or parenteral (B, C, D).`,`Hepatitis B chronicity is **90% perinatal**, about 30% at 1-5 years, **under 5% in adults**.`,`**55-85% of hepatitis C** infections become chronic.`,`Hepatitis D needs HBsAg; **superinfection of a carrier** causes rapid cirrhosis.`,`Chronic hepatitis B is staged by HBeAg, HBV DNA and ALT together (EASL phases).`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Liver injury is mainly **immune-mediated**: CD8 T cells kill infected hepatocytes.`,`Injured hepatocytes cannot excrete conjugated bilirubin, so urine darkens.`,`**INR rises first** in liver failure because factor VII has a 6-hour half-life.`,`Hepatitis B makes **cccDNA** in the nucleus, so drugs suppress it but rarely cure it.`,`Hepatitis C has no nuclear reservoir, so **12 weeks of drugs cures** it.`,`Chronic inflammation activates stellate cells, giving fibrosis and cirrhosis over 20-30 years.`,`**Precore mutants** in India replicate without HBeAg, so HBeAg-negative can still be active.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`A **5-7 day prodrome** with distaste for food and cigarettes precedes jaundice.`,`Fever usually settles as jaundice appears; persisting fever suggests another cause.`,`Ask about water source and **similar cases nearby** for hepatitis A or E.`,`Ask about transfusion, dialysis, **unsafe injections**, tattoos and drug use for B and C.`,`Ask about pregnancy and its trimester.`,`Ask about planned **rituximab, chemotherapy or high-dose steroids**, which can reactivate hepatitis B.`,`Ask the danger questions: vomiting, confusion, sleep reversal, bleeding, reduced urine.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Acute hepatitis gives a **mildly enlarged, smooth, tender liver**.`,`A **shrinking liver span** with deepening jaundice means massive necrosis.`,`Test for **asterixis** and orientation; encephalopathy with high INR is liver failure.`,`Look for gum bleeding and purpura from failed clotting factor synthesis.`,`Stigmata of chronic liver disease mean acute-on-chronic disease.`,`Check capillary glucose and hydration in anyone vomiting.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`LFT with **INR**; the INR, not the ALT, measures severity.`,`Acute panel: **IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HEV, HBsAg with IgM anti-HBc, anti-HCV**.`,`**Anti-HCV is only a screen**; confirm with HCV RNA before treatment.`,`New HBsAg-positive: HBeAg, **HBV DNA**, ALT, platelets, creatinine, anti-HDV, HIV and HCV.`,`**APRI over 2** or elastography over 12.5 kPa suggests cirrhosis.`,`HCV genotyping is not needed with pan-genotypic drugs.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Acute A, B and E are managed **supportively at home**; most recover in 4-8 weeks.`,`Normal balanced diet; **no fat-free diet, no glucose drips, no liver tonics**.`,`Stop alcohol completely; use ondansetron for vomiting and paracetamol for fever.`,`Chronic hepatitis B: **tenofovir DF 300 mg once daily**, long-term.`,`Hepatitis C: treat **everyone with viraemia** using sofosbuvir-velpatasvir for 12 weeks.`,`Hepatitis A and E: keep the patient off food handling for 1 week after jaundice.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Treat hepatitis B with **significant fibrosis**, or **HBV DNA over 2000 IU/mL with raised ALT**.`,`Also treat co-infection, family history of HCC, immunosuppression or diabetes (WHO 2024).`,`Use **tenofovir alafenamide** or entecavir in CKD, osteoporosis or age over 60.`,`**Never stop** tenofovir or entecavir without a specialist; flares can be severe.`,`Decompensated hepatitis C: sofosbuvir-velpatasvir plus **ribavirin** under a specialist.`,`Before rituximab or chemotherapy, give **entecavir or tenofovir prophylaxis** if HBsAg or anti-HBc positive.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Hepatitis E in the third trimester** has 15-25% maternal mortality; admit.`,`HBsAg-positive pregnancy: **TDF from 24-28 weeks** if HBV DNA 200000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg positive.`,`Baby of an HBsAg-positive mother: **HBIG plus birth-dose vaccine within 12 hours**.`,`Breastfeeding is safe for babies of HBsAg-positive mothers.`,`Hepatitis C drugs are deferred until after delivery.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NVHCP** started on 28 July 2018 to eliminate hepatitis C by 2030.`,`It gives **free testing and drugs** through Model Treatment Centres and Treatment Centres.`,`Hepatitis B **birth dose within 24 hours**, then pentavalent at 6, 10 and 14 weeks.`,`Adult vaccine **20 microgram IM in the deltoid at 0, 1 and 6 months**.`,`Anti-HBs **10 mIU/mL or more** after vaccination is protective.`,`No hepatitis E vaccine in India; prevention is safe water and sanitation.`,`Screen **every pregnant woman for HBsAg**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines for viral hepatitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate`,`300 mg oral once daily`,`Long-term; pregnancy from 24-28 weeks`,`Renal tubular injury, low phosphate, bone loss`],[`Tenofovir alafenamide`,`25 mg oral once daily with food`,`Long-term`,`Less renal and bone toxicity; raised lipids`],[`Entecavir`,`0.5 mg oral daily, empty stomach (1 mg if decompensated)`,`Long-term`,`Reduce if CrCl under 50; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Sofosbuvir + velpatasvir`,`400/100 mg oral, one tablet daily`,`12 weeks`,`Bradycardia with amiodarone; check HBsAg first`],[`Sofosbuvir + daclatasvir`,`400 mg + 60 mg oral daily`,`12 weeks; 24 weeks if cirrhosis`,`Adjust daclatasvir with CYP3A4 drugs`],[`Ribavirin`,`1000 mg/day (under 75 kg) or 1200 mg/day, divided`,`12-24 weeks`,`Haemolytic anaemia; teratogenic`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin`,`Newborn 0.5 mL IM; adult 0.06 mL/kg IM`,`Single dose; newborn within 12 h`,`Give at a different site from vaccine`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`Birth 10 microgram; adult 20 microgram IM`,`0, 1 and 6 months`,`Deltoid or thigh, never buttock`]]},{heading:`Hepatitis viruses compared`,columns:[`Virus`,`Route`,`Incubation`,`Chronicity`],rows:[[`Hepatitis A`,`Faeco-oral`,`15-50 days`,`Never`],[`Hepatitis B`,`Blood, sexual, perinatal`,`45-180 days`,`90% perinatal, under 5% adult`],[`Hepatitis C`,`Unsafe injection, transfusion, dialysis`,`15-150 days`,`55-85%`],[`Hepatitis D`,`Needs HBsAg`,`30-180 days`,`High after superinfection`],[`Hepatitis E`,`Water-borne`,`15-60 days`,`Only in immunosuppressed`]]},{heading:`Hepatitis B serology`,columns:[`Meaning`,`HBsAg`,`IgM anti-HBc`,`Anti-HBc total`,`Anti-HBs`],rows:[[`Acute infection`,`Positive`,`Positive`,`Positive`,`Negative`],[`Chronic infection`,`Positive over 6 months`,`Negative`,`Positive`,`Negative`],[`Window period`,`Negative`,`Positive`,`Positive`,`Negative`],[`Resolved infection`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Positive`,`Positive`],[`Vaccinated`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Positive`],[`Susceptible`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`]]}],remember:[`**A and E are enteric and never chronic**; B, C and D can become chronic.`,`Prognosis is read from **INR and sensorium**, never from the ALT.`,`Anti-HBs alone is vaccination; anti-HBs with anti-HBc is resolved infection.`,`HCV cure is **SVR12**: HCV RNA negative 12 weeks after treatment.`,`HBV drugs suppress for life; HCV drugs **cure in 12 weeks**.`,`Never give **amiodarone with sofosbuvir**.`,`HBeAg-negative does not mean inactive in India; check HBV DNA.`,`Birth dose within 24 hours; HBIG within 12 hours.`]},updated:[`AASLD-IDSA HBV 2025: treat immune-tolerant patients over 40 or with inflammation/fibrosis; shared decision-making for grey zone; stop NA only after HBsAg loss.`,`AASLD-IDSA HBV 2025: strong recommendation for TDF or TAF from 28 weeks of pregnancy when HBV DNA over 200000 IU/mL regardless of HBeAg.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - viral hepatitis A to E and NVHCP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Hepatocellular inflammation by hepatotropic viruses A-E; **chronic = HBsAg or HCV RNA positive beyond 6 months**; ALF = **INR 1.5+ plus encephalopathy**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Enteric A, E (never chronic) vs parenteral B, C, D (chronic)**; chronic HBV phases (EASL 2025) - HBeAg+/- chronic infection vs chronic hepatitis, occult; incubation A 15-50, E 15-60, B 45-180, C 15-150 days.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`A, E - contaminated water, monsoon outbreaks; B - **perinatal (90% chronic)**, sexual, unsafe injections, dialysis; C - unsafe injections, transfusion (Punjab, Haryana); D - superinfection of HBsAg carrier; HBsAg India **1-2%**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**CD8 T-cell killing** of infected hepatocytes -> necrosis -> conjugated bilirubin reflux; HBV **cccDNA in nucleus** (NA suppress, rarely cure); HCV cytoplasmic, no reservoir -> **DAA cure**; HEV pregnancy -> ALF.`],[`Key history`,`Prodrome, dark urine, water source and clusters, injections, transfusion, tattoos, sex, family HBV/HCC, pregnancy, herbal drugs, alcohol, **planned immunosuppression (rituximab, chemo)**, danger symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Icterus, hydration, **capillary glucose**, tender liver, **shrinking liver span**, asterixis, fetor, bleeding, CLD stigmata (acute-on-chronic), extrahepatic signs (purpura, PCT).`],[`Investigations`,`LFT + **INR**; **IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HEV, HBsAg + IgM anti-HBc, anti-HCV -> HCV RNA**; new HBsAg+: HBeAg, **HBV DNA, ALT, platelets, APRI/elastography**, anti-HDV, HIV, creatinine.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**IgM anti-HBc = acute/window**; anti-HBs alone = vaccinated; anti-HBs + anti-HBc = resolved; **APRI over 2 cirrhosis, over 0.5 significant fibrosis**; elastography **over 12.5 kPa cirrhosis**; **SVR12 = HCV cure**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Acute A/E supportive (normal diet, **ondansetron 4 mg**, paracetamol **max 2 g**); HBV **TDF 300 mg OD** (TAF 25 mg, entecavir 0.5 mg empty stomach); HCV **sofosbuvir 400 + velpatasvir 100 mg OD x 12 weeks**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`WHO 2024 treat: **fibrosis (APRI over 0.5)**, or **DNA over 2000 + ALT high** (ULN **30 men / 19 women**), co-infection, FHx HCC; **AASLD-IDSA 2025 adds immune-tolerant over 40**; decompensated HCV - **SOF-VEL + ribavirin 12 weeks**; HDV - peginterferon, bulevirtide.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`HBV vaccine **20 microgram deltoid 0, 1, 6 months** (never buttock), anti-HBs **10 mIU/mL+** protective; HBIG **0.06 mL/kg within 24 h** after needle-stick; ICU and transplant centre for ALF.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy HBsAg+: TDF from 24-28 weeks if DNA 200000+ or HBeAg+** (AASLD 2025: TDF/TAF from 28 weeks if DNA over 200000); newborn **HBIG 100 IU + birth dose within 12 h**; HEV third trimester **15-25% maternal mortality**; children 3+ get DAAs.`],[`Complications`,`ALF, cholestatic/relapsing HAV, chronic HBV/HCV -> **cirrhosis, HCC (even without cirrhosis in HBV)**, **HBV reactivation under rituximab**, extrahepatic (PAN, MGN, cryoglobulinaemia), amiodarone-sofosbuvir bradycardia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Encephalopathy, **INR 1.5+**, hypoglycaemia, persistent vomiting, bleeding, pregnancy with jaundice, shrinking liver; HBsAg+ before chemo/rituximab; patient who stopped tenofovir; refer decompensation, co-infection, DAA failure.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Acute: weekly bilirubin, ALT, INR; **HBsAg at 6 months**; on NA - DNA 3 then 6-12 monthly, **creatinine/phosphate on TDF**; **HCC USG 6-monthly** in cirrhosis and high-risk HBV; HCV RNA 12 weeks after therapy.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NVHCP (28 July 2018)** - free tests and drugs, HCV elimination by 2030; **birth dose within 24 h + pentavalent 6, 10, 14 weeks**; antenatal HBsAg screening; safe injections, blood screening; no HEV vaccine in India.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AASLD-IDSA HBV 2025**: treat immune-tolerant over 40 or with inflammation/fibrosis, shared decision in grey zone, **stop NA only after HBsAg loss**, **TDF/TAF from 28 weeks if DNA over 200000** (strong).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`A and E never chronic; **prognosis from INR and sensorium, not ALT**; anti-HCV stays positive after cure; HBeAg-negative is not inactive (precore mutants); never amiodarone with sofosbuvir; check HBsAg before DAAs.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`New HBsAg-positive patient: who gets tenofovir`,caption:`WHO 2024 expanded criteria with AASLD-IDSA 2025 additions.`,steps:[{label:`HBsAg positive`,detail:`HBeAg, HBV DNA, ALT, platelets, APRI/elastography, anti-HDV, HIV, HCV`},{label:`Cirrhosis or APRI over 0.5 / LSM over 7 kPa?`,detail:`Treat regardless of DNA and ALT`,tone:`decision`},{label:`DNA over 2000 IU/mL with raised ALT?`,detail:`ALT ULN 30 men, 19 women`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Co-infection, FHx HCC, immunosuppression, age over 40`,detail:`Treat (AASLD 2025: immune-tolerant over 40)`},{label:`Not meeting criteria`,detail:`ALT 6-12 monthly, DNA yearly; shared decision in grey zone`},{label:`TDF 300 mg OD long-term`,detail:`Never stop without specialist; stop only after HBsAg loss`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Preventing mother-to-child HBV - exam version`,steps:[{label:`Antenatal HBsAg in every pregnancy`,detail:`NVHCP universal screening`},{label:`HBsAg positive: check DNA/HBeAg`,detail:`DNA 200000+ or HBeAg+ (or test unavailable)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Tenofovir from 24-28 weeks`,detail:`TDF 300 mg OD till delivery; ALT 3 months after stopping`},{label:`Newborn within 12 hours`,detail:`HBIG 0.5 mL (100 IU) + birth-dose vaccine, different sites`},{label:`Complete pentavalent, test at 9-12 months`,detail:`HBsAg and anti-HBs; breastfeeding allowed`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Hepatitis B serology at a glance`,columns:[`State`,`HBsAg`,`IgM anti-HBc`,`Total anti-HBc`,`Anti-HBs`],rows:[[`Acute`,`+`,`+`,`+`,`-`],[`Chronic`,`+ over 6 months`,`-`,`+`,`-`],[`Window`,`-`,`+`,`+`,`-`],[`Resolved`,`-`,`-`,`+`,`+`],[`Vaccinated`,`-`,`-`,`-`,`+ (10 mIU/mL or more)`]]}]},"gastro-hepatology-chronic-liver-disease":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Cirrhosis** is diffuse fibrosis turning the liver into **regenerative nodules**.`,`It causes **portal hypertension** and **liver cell failure**.`,`**Compensated** cirrhosis has a median survival over 12 years.`,`**Decompensation** means ascites, variceal bleeding, overt encephalopathy or jaundice; survival falls to about 2 years.`,`In India the main causes are **alcohol, MASLD, hepatitis B and C**.`,`**Exclude Wilson disease** in any cirrhosis under 40, because it is treatable.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Child-Pugh**: Class A 5-6 points, B 7-9, C 10-15.`,`**MELD-Na 15 or more** is the trigger for transplant referral.`,`Liver stiffness over 15 kPa suggests compensated advanced chronic liver disease.`,`**Stiffness 25 kPa or more** rules in clinically significant portal hypertension (not in obese MASLD).`,`**Acute-on-chronic liver failure** is acute decompensation with organ failure; 28-day mortality exceeds 30%.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Chronic injury activates **hepatic stellate cells**, which lay down collagen.`,`Fibrosis and nodules raise resistance to portal blood flow.`,`Splanchnic vasodilation increases portal inflow, giving a **hyperdynamic circulation**.`,`Portal pressure opens collaterals such as **oesophageal varices** and caput medusae.`,`Low effective blood volume switches on **aldosterone**, so the kidney retains salt and water: **ascites**.`,`Gut bacteria translocate into ascites, causing **SBP**.`,`Ammonia bypasses the liver through shunts and swells astrocytes: **hepatic encephalopathy**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Quantify alcohol** in grams a day and years, and ask about the last drink.`,`Ask about viral risks, diabetes and obesity, and herbal or ayurvedic medicines.`,`Ask about abdominal swelling, leg swelling and **reduced urine**.`,`Ask about **haematemesis or black stools**; a variceal bleed may be silent melaena.`,`Ask relatives about **sleep reversal and confusion**; they notice encephalopathy first.`,`Ask about constipation, diuretic doses and vomiting, the common precipitants of encephalopathy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Hands: **palmar erythema**, leuconychia, clubbing, Dupuytren contracture and **asterixis**.`,`Chest: **spider naevi** (more than 5 is significant), gynaecomastia, loss of body hair.`,`**Splenomegaly** is the key sign of portal hypertension.`,`Confirm ascites by **shifting dullness**.`,`Caput medusae flows away from the umbilicus; IVC obstruction flows upwards.`,`A hard irregular liver or bruit suggests hepatocellular carcinoma.`,`Assess muscle wasting, because ascites hides weight loss.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, LFT with albumin, INR, creatinine and sodium give Child-Pugh and MELD-Na.`,`Low platelets under 150000 are often the **earliest clue** to cirrhosis.`,`**Ultrasound with portal Doppler** in every cirrhotic.`,`**Diagnostic tap in every new ascites**; no INR or platelet count forbids it.`,`**SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more** means portal hypertension.`,`**Ascitic PMN 250/mm3 or more** is SBP, whatever the culture.`,`Serum ammonia does **not** diagnose or monitor encephalopathy.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Complete abstinence from alcohol** is the most effective single treatment.`,`Salt restriction to **5 g salt a day**; no fluid restriction unless sodium under 125.`,`**Never restrict protein**: 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day with a late-evening snack.`,`Ascites: **spironolactone 100 mg with furosemide 40 mg** (ratio 100:40).`,`Target weight loss **0.5 kg/day without oedema**, 1 kg/day with oedema.`,`Clinically significant portal hypertension: **carvedilol 6.25 mg twice daily** (Baveno VIII strong).`,`Encephalopathy: **lactulose** to 2-3 soft stools a day, and find the precipitant.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Tense ascites: **large-volume paracentesis** with albumin 6-8 g per litre removed.`,`SBP: **cefotaxime 2 g IV 8-hourly** plus albumin 1.5 g/kg day 1 and 1 g/kg day 3.`,`After SBP, give **norfloxacin 400 mg daily** long-term as secondary prophylaxis.`,`Recurrent encephalopathy despite lactulose: **add rifaximin 550 mg twice daily**.`,`Hepatorenal syndrome: stop diuretics, give albumin, then **terlipressin plus albumin**.`,`Severe alcoholic hepatitis: **prednisolone 40 mg for 28 days**; stop if Lille 0.45 or more at day 7.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Stop diuretics if **sodium under 125**, creatinine over 2 mg/dL, or overt encephalopathy.`,`Fever, pain, confusion or rising creatinine with ascites means **tap for SBP the same day**.`,`Variceal bleed: terlipressin, ceftriaxone, and **band ligation within 12 hours**.`,`Avoid **NSAIDs, benzodiazepines, aminoglycosides** and ACE inhibitors in ascites.`,`Paracetamol up to **2-3 g a day** is the safest painkiller.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review compensated cirrhosis every **3-6 months** and recalculate Child-Pugh and MELD-Na.`,`**Ultrasound every 6 months** for liver cancer in every cirrhotic.`,`Check sodium, potassium and creatinine within 1 week of each diuretic change.`,`Refer for transplant at **first decompensation, MELD-Na 15 or more**, or Child B or C.`,`Vaccinate against hepatitis A and B, influenza and pneumococcus.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in cirrhosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Spironolactone`,`100 mg oral daily, up to 400 mg`,`Long-term`,`Hyperkalaemia, painful gynaecomastia`],[`Furosemide`,`40 mg oral daily, up to 160 mg`,`Long-term, with spironolactone`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI, encephalopathy`],[`Carvedilol`,`6.25 mg daily, then 6.25 mg BD`,`Long-term`,`Hypotension; hold if SBP under 90, AKI, SBP`],[`Lactulose`,`20-30 mL two to three times daily`,`Long-term after overt HE`,`Dehydration, hyponatraemia if overused`],[`Rifaximin`,`550 mg oral BD`,`Long-term for recurrent HE`,`Nausea; rare C. difficile`],[`Cefotaxime`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Allergy, C. difficile`],[`Albumin 20%`,`SBP 1.5 g/kg day 1, 1 g/kg day 3 IV`,`Per episode`,`Fluid overload`],[`Norfloxacin`,`400 mg oral daily`,`Long-term after SBP`,`Tendinopathy, QT, resistance`],[`Terlipressin`,`1 mg IV 4-6-hourly, with albumin`,`Up to 14 days`,`Gut, heart and digit ischaemia`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg oral daily`,`28 days`,`Infection, GI bleed; stop if Lille 0.45+`]]},{heading:`Child-Pugh score`,columns:[`Component`,`1 point`,`2 points`,`3 points`],rows:[[`Bilirubin (mg/dL)`,`Under 2`,`2-3`,`Over 3`],[`Albumin (g/dL)`,`Over 3.5`,`2.8-3.5`,`Under 2.8`],[`INR`,`Under 1.7`,`1.7-2.3`,`Over 2.3`],[`Ascites`,`None`,`Mild, diuretic-responsive`,`Moderate-tense or refractory`],[`Encephalopathy`,`None`,`Grade 1-2`,`Grade 3-4`]]},{heading:`West Haven grades of hepatic encephalopathy`,columns:[`Grade`,`Clinical features`,`Category`],rows:[[`Minimal`,`Abnormal psychometric tests only`,`Covert`],[`Grade 1`,`Short attention, sleep reversal, euphoria`,`Covert`],[`Grade 2`,`Lethargy, disorientation for time, asterixis`,`Overt`],[`Grade 3`,`Somnolent but rousable, grossly disoriented`,`Overt`],[`Grade 4`,`Coma`,`Overt`]]}],remember:[`First decompensation drops survival from **12 years to about 2 years**: refer for transplant.`,`Every new ascites gets a diagnostic tap; **no INR forbids it**.`,`**SAAG 1.1 or more** is portal hypertension; **PMN 250 or more** is SBP.`,`Spironolactone to furosemide **100:40**; salt 5 g a day.`,`**Never restrict protein** in encephalopathy.`,`Child-Pugh **A 5-6, B 7-9, C 10-15**; MELD 15 or more means transplant referral.`,`Ammonia does not diagnose encephalopathy; the precipitant matters.`]},updated:[`Baveno VIII 2026: CSPH ruled in by LSM 25 kPa or more (viral, alcohol, non-obese MASLD) or spleen stiffness over 55 kPa; not reliable in obese MASLD.`,`Baveno VIII 2026: NSBB (preferably carvedilol) for CSPH upgraded to a strong recommendation; no NSBB without CSPH; no TIPS to prevent decompensation.`,`ACG HE 2026: rifaximin added to lactulose in acute overt HE and for recurrence on lactulose; start 14 days before elective TIPS for 6 months.`,`ACG HE 2026: titrate lactulose with Bristol scale to 2-3 soft stools; high-volume PEG an inpatient alternative; lactulose for covert HE.`,`EASL-EASD-EASO MASLD 2024 / US FDA 2025: resmetirom and semaglutide 2.4 mg approved for non-cirrhotic MASH F2-F3.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - chronic liver disease and cirrhosis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Diffuse **fibrosis with regenerative nodules** -> portal hypertension + hepatocellular failure; **cACLD = LSM over 15 kPa**; **decompensation = ascites, variceal bleed, overt HE or jaundice** (median survival **over 12 years -> about 2 years**).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Child-Pugh A 5-6, B 7-9, C 10-15**; **MELD-Na 15+ -> transplant referral**; **West Haven HE grades**; **CSPH (Baveno VIII): LSM 25 kPa+ or spleen stiffness over 55 kPa**; APASL ACLF.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India: **alcohol 30-50%**, HBV, HCV, rising **MASLD**; Wilson (under 40), autoimmune, Budd-Chiari, cryptogenic; alcohol over **40-60 g/day men, 20 g/day women for 10 years**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Injury -> **TGF-beta -> stellate cell -> collagen** -> nodules -> **HVPG 10+ (CSPH), 12+ bleeds** -> splanchnic NO vasodilation -> low effective volume -> **RAAS/ADH -> ascites, hyponatraemia, HRS**; gut ammonia -> astrocyte glutamine -> HE.`],[`Key history`,`Alcohol in grams and last drink, viral and metabolic risks, drugs (NSAIDs, sedatives, herbals), distension, melaena, **sleep reversal/confusion (family)**, fever with ascites, cramps, transplant support.`],[`Key examination`,`Asterixis, palmar erythema, spider naevi (5+), gynaecomastia, **splenomegaly**, shifting dullness, caput medusae (flow away from umbilicus), oedema, wasting, KF rings, BP (**systolic under 90 limits NSBB**).`],[`Investigations`,`CBC (**platelets under 150000**), LFT, INR, creatinine, Na, K; HBsAg, anti-HCV; **USG with portal Doppler**; **diagnostic tap every new ascites/admission - SAAG 1.1+ = portal HTN, PMN 250+ = SBP**; elastography/FIB-4; endoscopy unless LSM under 20 + platelets over 150000 or on NSBB.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Clinical/imaging/LSM over 15 kPa (cACLD); **CSPH LSM 25 kPa+ (not in obese MASLD) or SSM over 55 kPa**; SBP PMN 250/mm3; AKI creatinine rise 0.3 mg/dL in 48 h or 50% in 7 days.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Stop cause (abstinence, antivirals)**; salt **5 g/day**, no fluid restriction unless Na under 125; protein **1.2-1.5 g/kg + late-evening snack**; **spironolactone 100 mg + furosemide 40 mg (100:40)** up to 400/160; **carvedilol 6.25 mg -> 6.25 mg BD for CSPH (strong, Baveno VIII)**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Tense ascites - **LVP + albumin 6-8 g/L removed** (over 5 L); SBP - **cefotaxime 2 g 8-hourly + albumin 1.5 g/kg day 1, 1 g/kg day 3**; HRS - **terlipressin 1 mg 4-6-hourly + albumin**; HE - **lactulose to 2-3 soft stools + rifaximin 550 mg BD**; AH - prednisolone 40 mg if DF 32+.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Paracentesis at any INR; **band ligation**; TIPS for refractory ascites/bleeding (**not to prevent first decompensation**); rifaximin **14 days before elective TIPS for 6 months**; transplant at MELD 15+.`],[`Special groups`,`Alcohol use disorder - **baclofen 5->10-25 mg TDS**, acamprosate if renal OK, avoid disulfiram/naltrexone; withdrawal - **lorazepam**; MASH F2-F3 - **resmetirom or semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly**; paracetamol **2-3 g/day** safe; avoid NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, benzodiazepines.`],[`Complications`,`Ascites, **SBP**, **HRS-AKI**, variceal bleed, **HE**, hyponatraemia, HCC (1-6%/year), PVT, hepatopulmonary syndrome, portopulmonary HTN, sarcopenia, ACLF.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Fever/pain/confusion with ascites (tap today), melaena, oliguria, **Na under 125 or K over 6**, grade 3-4 HE, ACLF after binge or HBV flare, new liver mass, cirrhosis under 40.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`3-6 monthly CBC, LFT, INR, creatinine, Child-Pugh/MELD-Na; electrolytes **within 1 week of diuretic change**; **USG +/- AFP 6-monthly for HCC**; endoscopy 2-yearly (no varices) if not on NSBB; depression and relapse screen.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NVHCP** free HBV/HCV treatment, HBV vaccine, **NP-NCD** metabolic screening, alcohol brief interventions; vaccinate hepatitis A/B, influenza, pneumococcal.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Baveno VIII 2026**: CSPH by LSM 25+ or **SSM over 55 kPa**, NSBB/carvedilol a **strong** recommendation, no TIPS for prevention; **ACG HE 2026**: rifaximin with lactulose in acute OHE, Bristol-guided lactulose, PEG alternative; **semaglutide FDA-approved for MASH (2025)**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Tap every new ascites; **SAAG 1.1+ = portal HTN**; PMN 250+ = SBP; **never restrict protein**; ammonia does not diagnose HE - find the precipitant; Child A 5-6 / B 7-9 / C 10-15; MELD 15 = refer.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Compensated cirrhosis: Baveno VIII risk pathway`,caption:`Non-invasive tests decide beta-blocker and endoscopy.`,steps:[{label:`Chronic liver disease: elastography + platelets`,detail:`LSM under 10 rules out cACLD; over 15 kPa = cACLD`},{label:`CSPH present?`,detail:`LSM 25+ (not obese MASLD) or spleen stiffness over 55 kPa`,tone:`decision`},{label:`CSPH - start carvedilol`,detail:`6.25 mg OD -> 6.25 mg BD; strong recommendation; no screening EGD needed`},{label:`No CSPH - no beta-blocker`,detail:`Treat cause; repeat NITs at 12 months if indeterminate`},{label:`Stop/hold NSBB if`,detail:`Systolic BP under 90, Na under 130, AKI, peritonitis (SBP) or HRS`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Decompensation prevented`,detail:`6-monthly USG for HCC; remove the cause`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Ascites management ladder`,steps:[{label:`Salt 5 g/day, stop NSAIDs/ACEi`,detail:`No fluid restriction unless Na under 125`},{label:`Spironolactone 100 mg (+ furosemide 40 mg)`,detail:`Weight loss 0.5 kg/day (1 kg with oedema)`},{label:`Step up 100:40 every 3-5 days`,detail:`Max spironolactone 400 mg, furosemide 160 mg`},{label:`Tense ascites: LVP + albumin`,detail:`6-8 g albumin per litre removed if over 5 L`},{label:`Refractory ascites`,detail:`Serial LVP, TIPS referral, transplant assessment`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Hepatic encephalopathy (ACG 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Find and correct the precipitant`,detail:`Bleed, infection/SBP, constipation, over-diuresis, low K/Na, sedatives`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Lactulose 25 mL every 1-2 h until stool`,detail:`Then titrate to 2-3 soft stools (Bristol scale); enema if comatose`},{label:`Add rifaximin 550 mg BD`,detail:`Suggested in acute OHE; recommended for recurrence on lactulose`},{label:`Inpatient alternative: high-volume PEG`,detail:`Instead of lactulose in overt HE`},{label:`Protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg + late snack`,detail:`No ammonia-guided therapy; refer for transplant`,tone:`good`}]}]},"gastro-hepatology-gi-bleeding":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The **ligament of Treitz** divides upper from lower GI bleeding.`,`Upper bleeding presents as **haematemesis, coffee-ground vomit or melaena**.`,`Lower bleeding presents as **haematochezia** (fresh red or maroon blood).`,`In India, upper bleeding is mostly **peptic ulcer (40-50%)** and **varices (20-30%)**.`,`About 10-15% of brisk red rectal bleeding with shock comes from an upper source.`,`**Shock index** (pulse divided by systolic BP) over 1 means significant blood loss.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`First decide **variceal or non-variceal**; it decides the drug and endoscopy timing.`,`**Glasgow-Blatchford score 0-1** is very low risk and can be managed as an outpatient.`,`Glasgow-Blatchford **2 or more** means admission.`,`**Forrest Ia, Ib and IIa** ulcers need endoscopic therapy.`,`Lower bleeding causes: piles, fissure, diverticula, angiodysplasia, cancer, colitis, and in India TB and amoebiasis.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the **gastroduodenal artery** and bleeds massively.`,`NSAIDs and aspirin block **COX-1**, removing mucosal protection and platelet function.`,`Clot fails below **pH 6**, so high-dose PPI stabilises the clot after endoscopy.`,`Varices bleed when the portal pressure gradient exceeds **12 mmHg**.`,`Over-transfusion raises portal pressure and causes **variceal rebleeding**.`,`Digested blood raises **urea with a normal creatinine** after an upper bleed.`,`Haemoglobin lags behind blood loss, so trust pulse and BP over the first Hb.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask what came out, how much, and whether stool is **black, tarry and foul**.`,`Ask about dizziness, fainting and chest pain; syncope adds 2 Blatchford points.`,`Ask the three questions: **liver disease, anticoagulant or antiplatelet, heart disease**.`,`Ask about NSAIDs, including over-the-counter and ayurvedic powders, and SSRIs.`,`Retching before the blood suggests a **Mallory-Weiss tear**.`,`Iron and bismuth blacken stool without tar or smell.`,`Rectal bleeding over 40 with changed bowel habit means **exclude colorectal cancer**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure pulse, lying and standing BP, capillary refill, urine output and conscious level.`,`A postural drop over 20 mmHg systolic means significant loss.`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** make the bleed variceal until proved otherwise.`,`Peritonism suggests perforation; a mass suggests cancer.`,`**Digital rectal examination in every patient**, then proctoscopy for rectal bleeding.`,`Surgical emphysema after vomiting means **Boerhaave rupture**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, crossmatch, **urea**, creatinine, LFT, INR and electrolytes.`,`Calculate **Glasgow-Blatchford** at the bedside before endoscopy.`,`Endoscopy **within 12 hours** for suspected varices or instability.`,`Endoscopy **within 24 hours** for all other upper bleeding.`,`Unstable lower bleeding: **CT angiography first**, then embolisation.`,`Stable lower bleeding: colonoscopy after bowel preparation.`,`Test for **H. pylori** in every bleeding ulcer.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Resuscitate before diagnosing**: two large-bore cannulae, oxygen, crystalloid boluses.`,`Transfuse only when **Hb is under 7 g/dL**, targeting 7-9 (8 in heart disease).`,`Suspected varices: give **terlipressin plus ceftriaxone** before transfer.`,`Non-variceal: **IV pantoprazole 80 mg**, but never delay endoscopy for it.`,`**Ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily for up to 7 days** in every cirrhotic with any bleed.`,`**No tranexamic acid** (HALT-IT) and **no FFP** to correct INR in cirrhosis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Oesophageal varices: **band ligation**; fundal varices: cyanoacrylate glue.`,`High-risk ulcer: **adrenaline plus a second method**, or an OTS clip; adrenaline alone rebleeds.`,`After ulcer therapy: high-dose PPI for 72 hours, then oral 40 mg twice daily to day 14.`,`Pre-emptive **TIPS within 72 hours** in Child C 10-13 or Child B with active bleeding.`,`Balloon tamponade is a bridge for **no more than 24 hours**.`,`After a variceal bleed: **beta-blocker plus repeated banding** until varices are obliterated.`,`Eradicate H. pylori with **14-day bismuth quadruple** and confirm cure.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Warfarin with major bleeding: **vitamin K 5-10 mg IV plus 4-factor PCC**.`,`Dabigatran: **idarucizumab 5 g IV**; apixaban or rivaroxaban: andexanet alfa where available.`,`Aspirin for secondary prevention: **restart within 3-5 days**; primary prevention aspirin stops.`,`Recent stent: discuss the P2Y12 inhibitor with the cardiologist and resume within 5 days.`,`Restart warfarin or a DOAC around **day 7** in most patients.`,`Child with painless large red stool: think **Meckel diverticulum**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Admit if **pulse over 100, systolic BP under 100**, syncope, cirrhosis, anticoagulant, or Blatchford 2 or more.`,`Re-scope every **gastric ulcer at 8-12 weeks** with biopsy.`,`Give iron for **3 months after Hb normalises**; check Hb at 2-4 weeks.`,`Rectal bleeding with changed bowel habit over 40, or iron-deficiency anaemia: **urgent cancer referral**.`,`Haematemesis after aortic graft surgery suggests **aorto-enteric fistula**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in GI bleeding`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Pantoprazole IV`,`80 mg bolus, then 8 mg/h (or 40 mg IV BD)`,`72 h, then oral 40 mg BD to day 14`,`Never delay endoscopy for it`],[`Terlipressin`,`2 mg IV 4-hourly, then 1 mg 4-hourly`,`2-5 days`,`Ischaemia, hyponatraemia; check sodium daily`],[`Octreotide`,`50 microgram IV bolus, then 50 microgram/h`,`2-5 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, bradycardia`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1 g IV once daily`,`Up to 7 days`,`Every cirrhotic with any bleed`],[`Propranolol`,`20-40 mg oral BD, to pulse 55-60`,`Long-term with banding`,`Hold if SBP under 90 or AKI`],[`Carvedilol`,`6.25 mg daily, then 6.25 mg BD`,`Long-term`,`Hypotension, dizziness`],[`Erythromycin IV`,`250 mg IV, 30-120 min before endoscopy`,`Single dose`,`QT prolongation`],[`Vitamin K + PCC`,`Vitamin K 5-10 mg IV, PCC 25-50 IU/kg`,`Once, for warfarin bleed`,`Thrombosis, anaphylactoid reaction`],[`Ferrous sulphate`,`200 mg oral daily or alternate days`,`3 months after Hb normal`,`Black stool, constipation`]]},{heading:`Forrest classification of bleeding ulcer`,columns:[`Grade`,`Finding`,`Management`],rows:[[`Ia`,`Spurting bleeding`,`Endoscopic therapy, high-dose PPI 72 h`],[`Ib`,`Oozing bleeding`,`Endoscopic therapy, high-dose PPI 72 h`],[`IIa`,`Non-bleeding visible vessel`,`Endoscopic therapy, high-dose PPI 72 h`],[`IIb`,`Adherent clot`,`Consider clot removal; high-dose PPI`],[`IIc`,`Flat pigmented spot`,`Oral PPI, early feeding`],[`III`,`Clean base`,`Oral PPI, early discharge`]]},{heading:`Variceal vs non-variceal upper bleeding`,columns:[`Feature`,`Variceal`,`Non-variceal (ulcer)`],rows:[[`Clues`,`Cirrhosis, ascites, splenomegaly`,`NSAIDs, dyspepsia, H. pylori`],[`Drug before endoscopy`,`Terlipressin plus ceftriaxone`,`IV pantoprazole`],[`Endoscopy timing`,`Within 12 hours`,`Within 24 hours`],[`Endoscopic therapy`,`Band ligation; glue for fundal`,`Adrenaline plus clip or thermal`],[`Secondary prevention`,`Beta-blocker plus banding`,`PPI, stop NSAID, eradicate H. pylori`]]}],remember:[`**Resuscitate first, diagnose second.**`,`Raised urea with normal creatinine means an **upper source**.`,`Transfuse below **Hb 7 g/dL**; over-transfusion makes varices rebleed.`,`Glasgow-Blatchford **0-1 goes home**; 2 or more is admitted.`,`Every cirrhotic bleed: **ceftriaxone plus terlipressin**.`,`Adrenaline alone is never enough for a high-risk ulcer.`,`No tranexamic acid and no FFP for INR in cirrhosis.`,`Secondary prevention aspirin restarts within 3-5 days.`]},updated:[`ESGE 2026: no emergent (6 h) or urgent (12 h) endoscopy for non-variceal bleeding unless unstable despite resuscitation; GBS 1 or less outpatient.`,`ESGE 2026: IV metoclopramide when erythromycin unavailable before endoscopy in severe bleeding.`,`ESGE 2026: OTS clip or haemostatic forceps monotherapy acceptable for high-risk stigmata; haemostatic powder not first-line monotherapy; OTS clip for rebleeding, then embolisation.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - upper and lower GI bleeding`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**UGIB** proximal to **ligament of Treitz** - haematemesis, coffee grounds, melaena; **LGIB** distal - haematochezia; **shock index (HR/SBP) over 1** = instability.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Variceal vs non-variceal**; **Glasgow-Blatchford** pre-endoscopy (0-1 outpatient, 2+ admit, 7+ needs intervention); **Forrest Ia, Ib, IIa, IIb, IIc, III**; Oakland 8 or less for LGIB discharge.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India: **peptic ulcer 40-50%, varices 20-30%**, erosions, Mallory-Weiss, cancer, Dieulafoy; LGIB - piles, fissure, **diverticula**, angiodysplasia, cancer, IBD, amoebic/TB colitis, Meckel in children; NSAIDs, aspirin, anticoagulants, SSRIs.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Posterior DU erodes **gastroduodenal artery**; platelets fail **below pH 6**, pepsin lyses clot below pH 5 -> PPI; HVPG 12+ -> variceal rupture; over-transfusion raises portal pressure; blood digestion -> **urea up, creatinine normal**.`],[`Key history`,`Volume and colour, syncope, **liver disease, anticoagulant/antiplatelet, heart disease**, NSAIDs and powders, retching first (Mallory-Weiss), weight loss, bowel habit change over 40, aortic graft.`],[`Key examination`,`Pulse, BP **lying and standing**, CRT, urine output, **shock index**, pallor, CLD stigmata and splenomegaly, abdominal mass, **DRE in every patient** + proctoscopy, emphysema (Boerhaave).`],[`Investigations`,`CBC, group and crossmatch, **urea, creatinine**, LFT, INR, glucose; **GBS** at bedside; endoscopy **within 12 h (varices/unstable) or 24 h**; **CT angiography first in unstable LGIB**; colonoscopy for stable LGIB; ECG/troponin in elderly.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Upper source likely with melaena, **urea:creatinine over 30**, haematemesis; GBS 0-1 very low risk; Forrest Ia-IIa high-risk stigmata needing endoscopic therapy.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Two 16-18G cannulae, crystalloid, **transfuse at Hb under 7 (target 7-9; 8 in IHD)**; non-variceal - **pantoprazole 80 mg IV**; variceal - **terlipressin 2 mg 4-hourly + ceftriaxone 1 g OD up to 7 days**; no tranexamic acid.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Post-therapy **PPI 80 mg then 8 mg/h x 72 h (or 40 mg IV BD)**, oral BD to day 14; rebleed -> **repeat endoscopy (OTS clip) -> TAE -> surgery**; variceal - **pre-emptive TIPS within 72 h (Child C 10-13, B over 7 active)**, rescue TIPS, balloon/stent bridge 24 h.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Erythromycin 250 mg IV 30-120 min before EGD** (metoclopramide if unavailable); **band ligation** oesophageal varices, **cyanoacrylate** fundal; ulcer - **adrenaline + clip/thermal, or OTS clip / forceps monotherapy**; powder only as rescue.`],[`Special groups`,`Warfarin - **vitamin K 5-10 mg IV + 4F-PCC 25-50 IU/kg**; DOAC - PCC 50 IU/kg, **idarucizumab 5 g** (dabigatran), andexanet; **secondary-prevention aspirin continue/restart in 3-5 days**; DAPT - discuss P2Y12 with cardiologist; child - Meckel, intussusception.`],[`Complications`,`Hypovolaemic shock, demand ischaemia, rebleeding, aspiration, **HE and infection in cirrhotics**, AKI, transfusion overload, iron deficiency.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pulse over 100, **SBP under 100**, syncope, cirrhosis signs, anticoagulants, brisk red bleeding with shock (upper source), rectal bleeding over 40 with habit change or IDA, Boerhaave, aorto-enteric fistula.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Hb at 2-4 weeks and 3 months, **iron 3 months after Hb normal**, **confirm H. pylori cure**, re-scope GU at 8-12 weeks; post-variceal - **NSBB + EVL every 1-4 weeks to obliteration**, then 3-6 then 6-12 monthly EGD.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`PPI with high-risk NSAID use, H. pylori test-and-treat before long-term aspirin, **carvedilol for CSPH (Baveno VIII)**, variceal screening, NG12-style colorectal referral.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ESGE 2026**: avoid urgent (12 h or less) endoscopy unless unstable after resuscitation; **OTS clip/forceps monotherapy allowed**; powder not first-line; **IV metoclopramide** if no erythromycin; OTS clip for rebleeding.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Resuscitate before diagnosis; **Hb 7 trigger**; every cirrhotic bleeder gets **ceftriaxone + terlipressin**; pre-endoscopy PPI never delays endoscopy; adrenaline alone is never enough; **no tranexamic acid (HALT-IT)**; no FFP to correct INR.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Upper GI bleed: first hour to endoscopy`,caption:`What the family physician does before the ambulance leaves.`,steps:[{label:`ABC, two large-bore lines, bloods`,detail:`CBC, crossmatch, urea, creatinine, INR, LFT`},{label:`Calculate Glasgow-Blatchford`,detail:`0-1 outpatient early EGD; 2+ admit`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Cirrhosis known or suspected?`,detail:`Ascites, splenomegaly, jaundice, alcohol`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Variceal: terlipressin + ceftriaxone`,detail:`Terlipressin 2 mg IV 4-hourly; ceftriaxone 1 g OD; EGD within 12 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Non-variceal: pantoprazole 80 mg IV`,detail:`EGD within 24 h; urgent only if unstable after resuscitation`},{label:`Restrictive transfusion, reverse anticoagulants`,detail:`Hb under 7; PCC for warfarin/DOAC`},{label:`Endoscopic haemostasis`,detail:`EVL / glue; ulcer dual or OTS clip therapy`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Variceal vs peptic ulcer bleed`,columns:[`Feature`,`Variceal`,`Peptic ulcer`],rows:[[`Clue`,`CLD stigmata, alcohol, HBV/HCV`,`NSAIDs, epigastric pain, H. pylori`],[`Drug before EGD`,`Terlipressin or octreotide + ceftriaxone`,`Pantoprazole 80 mg IV`],[`Endoscopy timing`,`Within 12 h`,`Within 24 h`],[`Endoscopic therapy`,`Band ligation; glue for fundal`,`Adrenaline + clip/thermal, or OTS clip`],[`Failure`,`Pre-emptive or rescue TIPS`,`Repeat EGD, embolisation, surgery`],[`Secondary prevention`,`NSBB + EVL to obliteration`,`Eradicate H. pylori, stop NSAID, PPI`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Rebleeding after ulcer haemostasis`,steps:[{label:`High-dose PPI 72 h`,detail:`80 mg bolus + 8 mg/h, or 40 mg IV BD`},{label:`Clinical rebleed: repeat endoscopy`,detail:`OTS clip preferred`},{label:`Fails: transcatheter arterial embolisation`,detail:`Interventional radiology`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Unavailable or fails: surgery`,detail:`Under-run the vessel`},{label:`Then oral PPI BD to day 14, eradicate H. pylori`,detail:`Test of cure 4 weeks later`,tone:`good`}]}]},"gastro-hepatology-ibs-constipation":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**IBS** is a disorder of gut-brain interaction: recurrent pain with altered bowel habit.`,`IBS is a **positive diagnosis**, not a diagnosis of exclusion.`,`**Rome V (2026)**: pain or discomfort on **at least 3 days a month for 3 months**.`,`The pain is **not continuous** and is linked to defecation or a change in stool.`,`Rome IV needed pain at least 1 day a week; Rome V brings back discomfort.`,`IBS affects about **4-7% of Indian adults**; chronic constipation affects 10-15%.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Subtype by **Bristol stool form** on abnormal days only.`,`**IBS-C**: over 25% Bristol 1-2; **IBS-D**: over 25% Bristol 6-7; **IBS-M**: both.`,`Constipation with prominent pain is IBS-C; without pain it is **functional constipation**.`,`Chronic constipation is normal-transit, slow-transit, or **dyssynergic defecation**.`,`Always exclude **secondary constipation** from drugs, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia and cancer.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The gut-brain axis miscommunicates, modulated by microbiota, immunity and stress.`,`**Visceral hypersensitivity** is the core: normal gas and stool cause pain.`,`Mucosal mast cells release histamine and tryptase that sensitise gut nerves.`,`The brain amplifies pain signals; anxiety and depression make it worse.`,`About **10% of bacterial gastroenteritis** is followed by post-infectious IBS.`,`**FODMAPs** draw water into the gut and ferment into gas, distending a sensitive gut.`,`In **dyssynergia**, the puborectalis paradoxically tightens on straining.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask how pain relates to defecation and how many days a month it occurs.`,`Ask the patient to **point to the Bristol chart**; words like 'loose' are unreliable.`,`Ask the **alarm questions**: onset over 45-50, bleeding, weight loss, nocturnal symptoms, fever.`,`Ask about milk, wheat, pulses and sorbitol sweets as triggers; use a food diary.`,`Take a drug history for **opioids, iron, calcium, amlodipine and anticholinergics**.`,`Ask about manual manoeuvres and sense of blockage, which suggest a defecatory disorder.`,`In India ask about evening fever and weight loss, which suggest **intestinal tuberculosis**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight and BMI**; documented weight loss is an alarm feature.`,`The abdomen is usually normal; a mass or distension suggests organic disease.`,`Inspect the perianal area for fissure, piles, fistula and perineal descent.`,`**Digital rectal examination is mandatory** in chronic constipation.`,`Ask the patient to push during DRE; **paradoxical tightening** suggests dyssynergia.`,`A positive **Carnett sign** suggests abdominal wall pain rather than gut pain.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Without alarm features: **CBC and CRP**, plus coeliac serology in IBS-D and IBS-M.`,`**Faecal calprotectin under 50 microgram/g** effectively excludes IBD; over 200 needs colonoscopy.`,`In India, check stool for **Giardia and Entamoeba** in diarrhoea-predominant symptoms.`,`Chronic constipation: **TSH, calcium, glucose and potassium**.`,`Colonoscopy only for alarm features, age 45-50 or over, or raised calprotectin.`,`Anorectal manometry with **balloon expulsion test** confirms dyssynergia.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Name the diagnosis** and say it does not cause cancer or shorten life.`,`Regular meals, 1.5-2 litres of fluid, less caffeine, and fruit limited to 3 portions a day.`,`**Soluble fibre (ispaghula)** helps; **insoluble bran worsens** pain and bloating.`,`Pain: **mebeverine 135 mg TDS** before meals, or **peppermint oil** capsules.`,`IBS-D: **loperamide 2 mg** before meals or outings.`,`Constipation: toilet 15-30 minutes after breakfast, with a **footstool** under the feet.`,`Constipation ladder: ispaghula, then **macrogol**, then senna or bisacodyl.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Low-FODMAP diet** for 4-6 weeks, then structured reintroduction.`,`Persistent pain: **amitriptyline 10 mg at night**, titrated to 30-50 mg.`,`Explain the tricyclic is **for gut pain signalling**, not because it is 'psychological'.`,`IBS-D with bloating: **rifaximin 550 mg TDS for 14 days**, repeatable twice.`,`IBS-C or refractory constipation: **linaclotide**, lubiprostone or **prucalopride 2 mg**.`,`Dyssynergia needs **biofeedback**, not more laxatives.`,`Refractory IBS: gut-directed CBT or hypnotherapy.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Children: disimpact with **macrogol 1-1.5 g/kg/day for 3-6 days**, then maintenance.`,`Pregnancy: ispaghula, lactulose or macrogol; avoid castor oil and liquid paraffin.`,`Elderly: macrogol plus senna; avoid magnesium in CKD and phosphate enemas in renal failure.`,`Opioid users: start a laxative with the opioid; **naloxegol** if refractory.`,`Faecal impaction: disimpact first; **never start bulk laxative** on an impacted rectum.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **4-6 weeks** and change one thing at a time.`,`Give amitriptyline 3 months and laxatives 4 weeks before judging failure.`,`**Re-evaluate, do not re-label**, when a new alarm feature appears.`,`Absolute constipation with vomiting and distension is **obstruction**: no laxatives, refer.`,`Refer refractory constipation after 3-6 months to a pelvic floor unit.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for IBS and constipation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mebeverine`,`135 mg oral TDS, 20 min before meals`,`Review at 4 weeks`,`Rare rash; safe in elderly`],[`Peppermint oil`,`0.2 mL enteric-coated, 1-2 capsules TDS`,`Review at 4 weeks`,`Heartburn if chewed`],[`Loperamide`,`2 mg before meals; max 16 mg/day`,`As needed`,`Constipation; not in bloody diarrhoea`],[`Rifaximin`,`550 mg oral TDS`,`14 days; may repeat twice`,`Nausea, rare C. difficile`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night, titrate to 30-50 mg`,`6-12 months if effective`,`Drowsiness, dry mouth; avoid in heart block`],[`Ispaghula husk`,`3.5 g once or twice daily in a full glass of water`,`Long-term`,`Bloating; avoid in impaction and opioid constipation`],[`Macrogol (PEG)`,`One 13.8-17 g sachet 1-3 times daily`,`Long-term`,`Bloating, loose stools`],[`Senna or bisacodyl`,`Senna 7.5-15 mg or bisacodyl 5-10 mg at night`,`Safe long-term`,`Cramps`],[`Linaclotide`,`290 microgram daily (IBS-C), before breakfast`,`Long-term`,`Diarrhoea; not under 2 years`],[`Prucalopride`,`2 mg daily (1 mg if over 65 or eGFR under 30)`,`Stop if no benefit at 4 weeks`,`Headache, nausea early`]]},{heading:`IBS subtypes and first choice`,columns:[`Subtype`,`Bristol pattern`,`First choice`],rows:[[`IBS-C`,`Over 25% type 1-2`,`Ispaghula, macrogol, linaclotide`],[`IBS-D`,`Over 25% type 6-7`,`Loperamide, rifaximin, amitriptyline`],[`IBS-M`,`Over 25% of both`,`Treat dominant symptom; antispasmodic`],[`IBS-U`,`Fits none of the above`,`Treat pain and current pattern`]]},{heading:`Bristol stool form scale`,columns:[`Type`,`Appearance`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`1`,`Separate hard lumps`,`Severe constipation`],[`2`,`Lumpy sausage`,`Constipation`],[`3-4`,`Smooth sausage, cracked or soft`,`Normal`],[`5`,`Soft blobs, clear edges`,`Tending to diarrhoea`],[`6`,`Mushy, ragged pieces`,`Diarrhoea`],[`7`,`Watery, no solids`,`Diarrhoea, fast transit`]]}],remember:[`IBS is a **positive diagnosis**: Rome V plus no alarm features.`,`Rome V: **3 days a month for 3 months**, not continuous.`,`**Soluble fibre helps, bran hurts**; lactulose bloats IBS.`,`Calprotectin **under 50** excludes IBD.`,`Low-dose amitriptyline is a **gut neuromodulator**; tell the patient.`,`Macrogol is the osmotic of choice; cathartic colon is a myth.`,`**Fibre without fluid** is why constipation treatment fails.`,`DRE with a push finds dyssynergia, which needs biofeedback.`]},updated:[`Rome V 2026: IBS = abdominal pain or discomfort on at least 3 days a month for 3 months, not continuous, linked to defecation or change in stool frequency/form (Rome IV needed pain 1 day a week).`,`Rome V 2026: separate short clinical criteria from research criteria; functional constipation criteria unchanged.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - IBS and chronic constipation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**IBS** - disorder of gut-brain interaction; **positive diagnosis, not exclusion**; **functional constipation** - 2+ of straining, hard stools, incomplete evacuation, blockage, manual manoeuvres, **under 3 SBM/week**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Rome V (2026)**: pain or **discomfort 3+ days a month for 3 months**, not continuous, linked to defecation or stool change; subtypes by **Bristol on abnormal days - IBS-C, IBS-D, IBS-M, IBS-U**; constipation - normal, slow transit, **dyssynergia**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Post-infectious (10% after bacterial gastroenteritis), stress, anxiety, FODMAPs, lactose, bile acid diarrhoea (25-30% IBS-D); constipation - **drugs (opioids, iron, CCBs, anticholinergics)**, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, low fibre/fluid.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Gut-brain axis -> **visceral hypersensitivity** (mast cells, TRPV1) + central amplification -> altered motility; FODMAP -> osmotic water + gas; dyssynergia -> **paradoxical puborectalis contraction**.`],[`Key history`,`Pain/discomfort relation to defecation, **Bristol chart**, **alarm features (onset over 45-50, bleeding, weight loss, nocturnal symptoms, anaemia, FHx CRC/IBD/coeliac)**, diet diary, drugs, manual manoeuvres, PHQ-2/GAD-2.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, pallor, thyroid, abdominal mass, **Carnett sign**, perianal inspection, **DRE with push manoeuvre** for dyssynergia, neurological signs, child's spine.`],[`Investigations`,`**CBC, CRP, tTG-IgA + total IgA**; **faecal calprotectin under 50 microgram/g excludes IBD** (over 200 colonoscopy); stool for Giardia/Entamoeba in India; constipation - **TSH, calcium, glucose**; manometry + balloon expulsion if refractory.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Rome V clinical criteria + no alarm features + normal minimal tests; IBS-C over 25% Bristol 1-2 and under 25% 6-7 (IBS-D reverse); balloon not expelled in 1-2 min = dyssynergia; transit over 20% markers at day 5 = slow transit.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Explain and reassure; regular meals, fluid **1.5-2 L**, **soluble fibre (ispaghula 3.5 g OD-BD)**, avoid bran; pain - **mebeverine 135 mg TDS** or **peppermint oil 0.2 mL TDS**; IBS-D - **loperamide 2 mg**; constipation - **macrogol 13.8-17 g OD**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Low-FODMAP 4-6 weeks then reintroduction**; **amitriptyline 10 mg -> 30-50 mg at night**; **rifaximin 550 mg TDS x 14 days** (IBS-D); stimulant **senna 7.5-15 mg / bisacodyl 5-10 mg**; **linaclotide 290 microgram** (IBS-C), **prucalopride 2 mg** (1 mg if over 65).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Footstool posture (knees above hips)**, toilet 15-30 min after breakfast; **biofeedback for dyssynergia**; gut-directed CBT/hypnotherapy; disimpaction with **macrogol 8 sachets/day up to 3 days** or enemas.`],[`Special groups`,`Child - **macrogol 1-1.5 g/kg/day x 3-6 days then 0.4-0.8 g/kg/day for 2+ months**; pregnancy - ispaghula, lactulose, macrogol, short senna; elderly - macrogol + senna, avoid Mg and phosphate enemas in CKD; opioids - **naloxegol 25 mg**; linaclotide not under 2 years.`],[`Complications`,`Impaction with overflow, fissure, haemorrhoids, rectal prolapse, poor quality of life, doctor-shopping, missed organic disease (TB, coeliac, cancer).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Onset over 45-50**, rectal bleeding, IDA, weight loss, nocturnal symptoms, mass, **calprotectin over 200**, absolute constipation with vomiting (obstruction); refer refractory constipation after 3-6 months.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **4-6 weeks**, one change at a time; laxatives/antispasmodics 4-week trial, neuromodulators 3 months (continue 6-12 months); **re-evaluate, do not re-label** when pattern changes.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Fibre 25-30 g/day with fluids, activity 150 min/week (yoga), early treatment of dysentery, avoid unnecessary constipating drugs.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Rome V 2026**: **discomfort returns, frequency 3 days a month** (not weekly), pain must not be continuous, **clinical vs research criteria** separated; functional constipation unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`IBS is a positive diagnosis; **nocturnal symptoms are never IBS**; lactulose bloats IBS; **cathartic colon is a myth**; DRE push test finds dyssynergia; low-dose amitriptyline is for gut pain - say so.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`IBS: making the positive diagnosis (Rome V)`,steps:[{label:`Pain or discomfort 3+ days a month x 3 months`,detail:`Not continuous; linked to defecation or stool change`},{label:`Alarm features?`,detail:`Over 45-50, bleeding, weight loss, nocturnal, anaemia, FHx`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - colonoscopy / investigate`,detail:`Not an IBS label`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No - minimal tests`,detail:`CBC, CRP, tTG-IgA, calprotectin, stool for Giardia`},{label:`Subtype by Bristol on abnormal days`,detail:`IBS-C, IBS-D, IBS-M, IBS-U`},{label:`Name it, explain, treat dominant symptom`,detail:`Review 4-6 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Chronic constipation ladder`,steps:[{label:`Stop constipating drugs; fibre + fluid + posture`,detail:`Fibre 25-30 g, footstool, toilet after breakfast`},{label:`Bulk: ispaghula 3.5 g OD-BD`,detail:`In 200-250 mL water; not in impaction or opioids`},{label:`Osmotic: macrogol 13.8-17 g OD (to 3 sachets)`,detail:`Lactulose 15-30 mL cheaper but bloats`},{label:`Stimulant: senna 7.5-15 mg / bisacodyl 5-10 mg`,detail:`At night; safe long-term`},{label:`Secretagogue/prokinetic`,detail:`Linaclotide 145-290 microgram, prucalopride 2 mg`},{label:`Refractory: manometry, biofeedback`,detail:`Dyssynergia needs biofeedback, not laxatives`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`IBS drug by subtype`,root:`Dominant symptom`,arms:[{label:`Pain`,steps:[`Mebeverine 135 mg TDS`,`Peppermint oil 0.2 mL TDS`,`Amitriptyline 10-50 mg at night`]},{label:`IBS-D`,steps:[`Loperamide 2 mg`,`Rifaximin 550 mg TDS x 14 days`,`Ondansetron 4-8 mg`,`Bile acid sequestrant`]},{label:`IBS-C`,steps:[`Ispaghula`,`Macrogol`,`Linaclotide 290 microgram`,`SSRI if anxiety`]}]}]},"gastro-hepatology-acute-abdomen-pancreatitis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The **acute abdomen** is pain of under 1 week needing an urgent decision on surgery or admission.`,`At first contact the job is **triage and resuscitation**, not exact diagnosis.`,`**Acute appendicitis** is the commonest surgical acute abdomen.`,`Acute pancreatitis needs **2 of 3**: typical pain, **lipase or amylase 3x normal or more**, imaging.`,`Pancreatitis causes: **gallstones 40-50%, alcohol 30-35%**, then triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL.`,`**Charcot triad** of fever with rigors, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain means cholangitis.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by mechanism: inflammation, perforation, obstruction, vascular, colic, gynaecological, medical mimics.`,`**Alvarado 7 or more** refer; 5-6 image or observe; 4 or less unlikely.`,`Atlanta **mild**: no organ failure and no complications.`,`**Moderately severe**: organ failure resolving within 48 hours, or local complications.`,`**Severe**: organ failure persisting **beyond 48 hours**; mortality 20-30%.`,`Organ failure is a **modified Marshall score of 2 or more**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Visceral pain is dull and midline; parietal pain is sharp and localised.`,`Appendicitis pain **migrates from umbilicus to McBurney point** as the peritoneum inflames.`,`In pancreatitis, **trypsinogen activates early** inside acinar cells and the gland digests itself.`,`Released cytokines cause SIRS, capillary leak and **haemoconcentration**.`,`Hypovolaemia in the first 24-48 hours can turn oedematous pancreatitis into necrosis.`,`Starvation lets gut bacteria translocate, so **early feeding** prevents infected necrosis.`,`Enzyme height reflects release, so **it does not predict severity**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Use **SOCRATES**; sudden onset suggests perforation, rupture, torsion or ischaemia.`,`Migration of pain to the right iliac fossa with anorexia suggests appendicitis.`,`Ask about vomiting, distension and **absolute constipation** for obstruction.`,`Ask **every woman the last menstrual period**; ruptured ectopic is the avoidable death.`,`Ask about alcohol, gallstones and drugs such as azathioprine and valproate.`,`Elderly, diabetic, pregnant and immunosuppressed patients have **blunted signs**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Start with **ABC and vital signs**; shock means transfer now.`,`A **restless** patient has colic; a patient **lying still** has peritonitis.`,`Guarding, rigidity and **percussion tenderness** mean peritonitis.`,`Appendicitis signs: McBurney tenderness, **Rovsing, psoas and obturator** signs.`,`**Murphy sign** indicates acute cholecystitis.`,`Examine **all hernial orifices** and the testes in every acute abdomen.`,`Grey Turner and Cullen bruising are late signs of severe pancreatitis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Urine pregnancy test** in every woman of reproductive age.`,`**ECG** in anyone over 40 with upper abdominal pain; check glucose and ketones.`,`**Erect chest X-ray** shows free gas in 70-80% of perforations.`,`**Ultrasound** is first-line; every pancreatitis patient needs one for gallstones.`,`Delay CT in pancreatitis to **72-96 hours** unless the diagnosis is in doubt.`,`Severity: haematocrit over 44%, rising urea, CRP over 150 at 48 hours, **BISAP 3 or more**.`,`Check fasting triglycerides and calcium to find the cause.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Resuscitate: oxygen, **two large-bore cannulae**, crystalloid bolus in shock.`,`**Never withhold analgesia** while awaiting the surgeon; it does not mask peritonism.`,`**Diclofenac 75 mg IM or IV** is first-line for biliary and ureteric colic.`,`Pancreatitis: **Ringer lactate 10 mL/kg bolus** if hypovolaemic, then 1.5 mL/kg/hour.`,`Pancreatitis: **low-fat oral diet within 24-48 hours** as pain allows.`,`**No prophylactic antibiotics** in pancreatitis, even when necrotising.`,`Never give a purgative or discharge an undiagnosed acute abdomen on antibiotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Infected necrosis: **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly**, then a step-up drainage approach.`,`Gallstone pancreatitis: **ERCP within 24 hours only with cholangitis**.`,`Mild gallstone pancreatitis: **same-admission cholecystectomy** prevents 25-30% recurrence.`,`Cholecystitis: antibiotics and laparoscopic cholecystectomy **within 72 hours**.`,`Uncomplicated appendicitis: laparoscopic appendicectomy, or **antibiotics alone** after shared decision.`,`Triglycerides over 1000: **insulin infusion with dextrose**, later fenofibrate.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Transfer **immediately** for shock, generalised peritonitis, free gas, ectopic or aneurysm rupture.`,`Pain **out of proportion to signs** in an AF patient means mesenteric ischaemia.`,`A tender irreducible groin lump with vomiting is a **strangulated hernia**.`,`Pancreatitis complications: **ARDS, AKI, hypocalcaemia**, hyperglycaemia and abdominal compartment syndrome.`,`Avoid over 4 L of fluid in 24 hours; aggressive fluids cause overload (**WATERFALL**).`,`Avoid NSAIDs after 20 weeks of pregnancy; paracetamol and opioids are safe.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review undiagnosed well patients **within 4-6 hours** or next morning, with written safety-netting.`,`After pancreatitis, review at **4-6 weeks** and check HbA1c yearly.`,`Drain a pseudocyst **only if symptomatic or infected**, not by size alone.`,`Idiopathic pancreatitis over 40 needs **EUS or MRCP** to exclude cancer.`,`Give a brief alcohol intervention during admission to reduce recurrence.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in the acute abdomen and pancreatitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`1 g IV or oral 6-hourly; max 4 g/day`,`Regular while in pain`,`2-3 g/day in liver disease`],[`Morphine`,`0.1 mg/kg IV, titrated 2-3 mg every 5 min`,`As needed`,`Respiratory depression; keep naloxone 0.4 mg`],[`Diclofenac`,`75 mg IM or IV, or 100 mg PR`,`Once; repeat after 12 h`,`AKI, GI bleed; avoid late pregnancy`],[`Hyoscine butylbromide`,`20 mg IV or IM; max 100 mg/day`,`As needed for colic`,`Avoid in glaucoma and ileus`],[`Ringer lactate`,`10 mL/kg bolus, then 1.5 mL/kg/h`,`Reassess at 3, 12, 24, 48 h`,`Overload; avoid over 4 L in 24 h`],[`Ceftriaxone + metronidazole`,`2 g IV daily + 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`4-7 days`,`Not as pancreatitis prophylaxis`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`For infected necrosis`,`Seizures at high dose; renal dosing`],[`Tamsulosin`,`0.4 mg oral at night`,`Up to 4 weeks`,`Postural hypotension`],[`Pancrelipase`,`25000-50000 lipase units per meal`,`Long-term if insufficiency`,`Take with first bite; swallow whole`]]},{heading:`Revised Atlanta severity of acute pancreatitis`,columns:[`Grade`,`Definition`,`Action`],rows:[[`Mild`,`No organ failure or complications`,`Ward care, early feeding; mortality under 1%`],[`Moderately severe`,`Organ failure resolving within 48 h, or local complications`,`HDU monitoring; CT at 72-96 h`],[`Severe`,`Organ failure persisting beyond 48 h`,`ICU; mortality 20-30%`]]},{heading:`Alvarado score (MANTRELS)`,columns:[`Component`,`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`Migration`,`Pain moves to right iliac fossa`,`1`],[`Anorexia`,`Present`,`1`],[`Nausea`,`Nausea or vomiting`,`1`],[`Tenderness`,`Right iliac fossa`,`2`],[`Rebound`,`Present`,`1`],[`Elevated temperature`,`37.3 C or more`,`1`],[`Leucocytosis`,`WBC over 10000`,`2`],[`Shift to left`,`Neutrophils over 75%`,`1`]]}],remember:[`First contact is **triage and resuscitation**, not diagnosis.`,`**Colic is restless; peritonitis lies still.**`,`Pregnancy test in every woman; **ECG over 40** with upper abdominal pain.`,`Alvarado mnemonic **MANTRELS**: 7 or more refer.`,`Pancreatitis is **2 of 3**; enzyme height does not predict severity.`,`Severe Atlanta means organ failure **beyond 48 hours**.`,`Moderate Ringer lactate, early feeding, **no prophylactic antibiotics**.`,`ERCP only with cholangitis; same-admission cholecystectomy for mild gallstone pancreatitis.`]},updated:[`WSES 2025 (JAMA Surg 2026): clinical scores recommended for risk stratification - AIR score or Adult Appendicitis Score preferred over Alvarado.`,`WSES 2025: antibiotics-first for imaging-confirmed uncomplicated appendicitis (appendicolith or diameter 7 mm or more predicts failure); post-operative antibiotics 2-3 days in complicated appendicitis.`,`WSES 2025: after non-operative treatment of appendiceal abscess, colonoscopy/CT for neoplasm; no routine interval appendicectomy.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - acute abdomen and acute pancreatitis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Acute abdomen** - pain under 1 week needing urgent decision on surgery/admission; **acute pancreatitis = 2 of 3**: typical pain, **lipase/amylase 3x ULN or more**, imaging.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Mechanism - inflammation, perforation, obstruction, vascular, colic, gynaecological, medical mimics; **revised Atlanta: mild, moderately severe (organ failure under 48 h or local complications), severe (over 48 h)**; **AIR/Adult Appendicitis Score, Alvarado 7+**; Tokyo 2018 cholangitis.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Pancreatitis - **gallstones 40-50%, alcohol 30-35%**, TG over 1000 mg/dL, hypercalcaemia, drugs (azathioprine, valproate, GLP-1 RA), post-ERCP, scorpion sting; appendicitis - faecolith; elderly, diabetic, pregnant present atypically.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pancreas: **intra-acinar trypsinogen -> trypsin** -> autodigestion -> IL-6/TNF -> **SIRS, capillary leak, haemoconcentration** -> hypoperfusion -> necrosis; appendix: obstruction -> ischaemia -> **visceral (umbilical) -> parietal (RIF) pain** -> perforation 24-72 h.`],[`Key history`,`SOCRATES, sudden vs gradual, migration to RIF, vomiting/distension/absolute constipation order, **LMP and pregnancy in every woman**, alcohol and gallstones, drugs, previous surgery, AF, diabetes, last meal.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals + **NEWS2/qSOFA**, restless (colic) vs still (peritonitis), guarding, rigidity, McBurney/Rovsing/psoas/obturator, Murphy, **all hernial orifices**, pulsatile mass, bowel sounds, PR/PV, testes; Grey Turner, Cullen.`],[`Investigations`,`**UPT, glucose/ketones, urine dipstick, ECG over 40**; CBC, urea, creatinine, **lipase**, LFT, calcium, TG, CRP, lactate; **erect CXR** (free gas 70-80%); **USG first** (gallstones in every pancreatitis); **CT at 72-96 h** only if doubt or not improving.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Pancreatitis 2 of 3; severity - **SIRS over 48 h, Hct over 44%, BUN over 20 rising, CRP over 150 at 48 h, BISAP 3+**; organ failure **modified Marshall 2+**; appendicitis - AIR/AAS or Alvarado 7+ refer, 5-6 image/observe.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Resuscitate, **analgesia never withheld** (paracetamol 1 g 6-hourly, **morphine 0.1 mg/kg titrated**, diclofenac 75 mg for colic); pancreatitis **RL 10 mL/kg bolus if hypovolaemic then 1.5 mL/kg/h**, reassess, **oral low-fat diet in 24-48 h**, **no prophylactic antibiotics**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Infected necrosis - **meropenem 1 g 8-hourly** + **step-up drainage after 4 weeks**; cholangitis - pip-taz + **ERCP within 24 h**; HTG - **insulin 0.1-0.3 U/kg/h + dextrose**; appendicitis - laparoscopic appendicectomy or **antibiotics-first if uncomplicated** (WSES 2025).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`NG tube for vomiting/obstruction, catheter in shock, **same-admission cholecystectomy** after mild gallstone pancreatitis, ERCP only with cholangitis, early lap cholecystectomy within 72 h for cholecystitis, post-op antibiotics **2-3 days** in complicated appendicitis.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - **UPT, ectopic**, appendix displaced upward, no NSAIDs after 20 weeks; elderly/diabetic - blunted signs, ECG, mesenteric ischaemia; child - intussusception, malrotation, torsion; HTG pancreatitis - fenofibrate after.`],[`Complications`,`Pancreatitis: **ARDS, AKI, hypocalcaemia**, necrosis, infected necrosis, pseudocyst/WON, abdominal compartment syndrome, diabetes, exocrine insufficiency; appendicitis: perforation, abscess; obstruction: strangulation.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Shock, rigidity, free gas, **positive UPT with pain or faint**, pain out of proportion (ischaemia), Charcot/Reynolds, irreducible hernia, pulsatile mass, pancreatitis with persistent SIRS or rising urea.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Undiagnosed but well - review 4-6 h or next morning with safety-netting; post-pancreatitis **4-6 weeks**, HbA1c yearly, faecal elastase; idiopathic over 40 - EUS/MRCP; after NOM of appendiceal abscess - **colonoscopy/CT**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Alcohol brief intervention, smoking cessation, cholecystectomy for symptomatic stones, TG control under 500 mg/dL, low threshold referral in elderly and pregnant.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WSES 2025 appendicitis**: **AIR or Adult Appendicitis Score** for risk stratification, antibiotics-first for uncomplicated disease (appendicolith/7 mm+ predicts failure), **2-3 day post-op antibiotics** in complicated, colonoscopy after abscess NOM.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Triage, not diagnosis; **UPT in every woman, ECG over 40**; enzyme height does not predict severity; **moderate fluids (WATERFALL)**; feed early; no prophylactic antibiotics; ERCP only with cholangitis.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute abdomen at first contact`,steps:[{label:`Vitals, NEWS2, analgesia`,detail:`Paracetamol 1 g + titrated morphine; never withhold`},{label:`Shock, peritonitis, free gas?`,detail:`Or positive UPT with faint, pulsatile mass, ischaemia`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - transfer now, resuscitate en route`,detail:`Two lines, O2, NG, antibiotics if septic`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Bedside: UPT, glucose/ketones, dipstick, ECG`,detail:`Lipase, CBC, CRP, USG`},{label:`Specific surgical diagnosis?`,detail:`Appendicitis (AIR/AAS), cholecystitis, pancreatitis - same-day transfer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Well and undiagnosed`,detail:`Review in 4-6 h with written safety-net`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute pancreatitis: first 72 hours`,steps:[{label:`2 of 3: pain, lipase 3x, imaging`,detail:`No CT needed if pain + enzyme`},{label:`Severity markers`,detail:`SIRS, Hct over 44%, BUN rising, BISAP 3+`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Moderate goal-directed Ringer lactate`,detail:`10 mL/kg if hypovolaemic, then 1.5 mL/kg/h; avoid over 4 L/24 h`},{label:`Analgesia + oral low-fat diet in 24-48 h`,detail:`No prophylactic antibiotics, no octreotide`},{label:`Cholangitis?`,detail:`ERCP within 24 h; otherwise no ERCP`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Find cause, same-admission cholecystectomy`,detail:`USG, TG, calcium; CT at 72-96 h if not improving`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Revised Atlanta severity`,columns:[`Grade`,`Organ failure`,`Complications`,`Mortality / care`],rows:[[`Mild`,`None`,`None`,`Under 1%; ward`],[`Moderately severe`,`Transient, under 48 h`,`Local or systemic may be present`,`Low; HDU`],[`Severe`,`Persistent, over 48 h`,`Usually present`,`20-30%; ICU tertiary`]]}]},"gastro-hepatology-liver-abscess":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **liver abscess** is a collection of pus or necrotic material in the liver.`,`The classification that matters is **amoebic versus pyogenic**.`,`**Amoebic** is commonest in India: young men aged 20-50 who drink alcohol.`,`**Pyogenic** affects older, **diabetic** patients with biliary disease.`,`Treated amoebic abscess has mortality **under 1-3%**; pyogenic stays 5-10%.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Amoebic abscess is caused by **Entamoeba histolytica** from contaminated water or food.`,`**Alcohol**, especially country liquor and toddy, is the strongest amoebic risk factor.`,`Pyogenic abscess comes most often by the **biliary route**: stones, stricture, stents, cancer.`,`Other pyogenic routes: portal (appendicitis, diverticulitis), arterial, direct; a fifth are cryptogenic.`,`Pyogenic organisms: **E. coli, Klebsiella**, Streptococcus milleri and anaerobes.`,`Diabetes predisposes to **hypervirulent Klebsiella** that spreads to the eye and brain.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Swallowed amoebic cysts **resist routine chlorination** and become trophozoites in the colon.`,`Trophozoites invade the colon, often without any dysentery.`,`They reach the liver by the **portal vein**, mostly the **posterosuperior right lobe**.`,`Liquefied liver forms sterile **anchovy-sauce pus**; amoebae live only in the wall.`,`The abscess stretches Glisson capsule and irritates the diaphragm, giving shoulder-tip pain.`,`Pyogenic pus needs drainage because antibiotics penetrate thick pus poorly.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about **fever and right upper quadrant pain**, worse on breathing or lying on the right.`,`Amoebic abscess is subacute over **1-2 weeks**; pyogenic is acute with rigors.`,`Take an **alcohol history**; alcohol also reacts with metronidazole.`,`Most amoebic patients give **no history of dysentery**.`,`Ask about diabetes, gallstones, biliary procedures and appendicitis.`,`Ask about cough with brown sputum, and blurred vision in a diabetic.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Tender hepatomegaly** is the main sign.`,`**Intercostal tenderness** in the right lower spaces is characteristic of amoebic abscess.`,`Check the right lung base for dullness or crackles.`,`Jaundice suggests a biliary pyogenic abscess.`,`An epigastric mass suggests a **left-lobe abscess**; listen for a pericardial rub.`,`Check vital signs; hypotension means sepsis or rupture.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC shows neutrophil leucocytosis; eosinophilia suggests hydatid instead.`,`**Alkaline phosphatase** is the commonest raised liver test.`,`**Ultrasound** is the first imaging: a hypoechoic lesion against the right lobe capsule.`,`Chest X-ray shows a **raised right hemidiaphragm**.`,`**Amoebic IgG serology** is over 90% positive; a negative result argues against amoebic.`,`**Blood cultures before antibiotics** in suspected pyogenic abscess.`,`Stool microscopy is not useful; it is positive in only a minority.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Amoebic: **metronidazole 750 mg three times daily for 7-10 days**.`,`Give metronidazole IV 500 mg 8-hourly if vomiting or toxic.`,`**Then always a luminal agent**: paromomycin or diloxanide furoate.`,`**No alcohol** during and for 72 hours after metronidazole.`,`Unclear type: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus metronidazole**.`,`Pyogenic: **drainage plus 4-6 weeks of antibiotics plus source control**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Expect improvement within **72-96 hours**; failure means drainage, not a drug switch.`,`Drain if **over 5-10 cm**, thin rim of liver, **left lobe**, or impending rupture.`,`Needle aspiration suits abscesses under 5 cm; larger ones need a catheter.`,`Biliary source: **ERCP** with stone clearance or stenting.`,`ESBL risk: **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly**, adjusted to culture.`,`Surgery only for rupture with peritonitis or failed percutaneous drainage.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Pleuropulmonary spread is the commonest** complication.`,`Anchovy-sauce sputum means a **hepatobronchial fistula**.`,`**Pericardial rupture** from a left-lobe abscess is the deadliest.`,`Sudden pain with guarding and shock means **peritoneal rupture**.`,`Blurred vision in a diabetic suggests **Klebsiella endophthalmitis**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **72 hours**, then weekly until well.`,`The amoebic cavity takes **3-12 months** to resolve; treat the patient, not the scan.`,`After Klebsiella or Strep milleri abscess, arrange **colonoscopy**.`,`**Boil drinking water**; amoebic cysts resist routine chlorination.`,`Treat household cyst passers with a luminal agent; notify clusters through IDSP-IHIP.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for liver abscess`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Metronidazole`,`750 mg oral TDS (500 mg IV 8-hourly); child 35-50 mg/kg/day`,`7-10 days`,`Metallic taste, neuropathy; no alcohol; raises INR`],[`Tinidazole`,`2 g oral once daily with food`,`3-5 days`,`Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol`],[`Paromomycin`,`25-35 mg/kg/day in 3 doses with meals`,`7 days, after tissue course`,`Loose stools, cramps`],[`Diloxanide furoate`,`500 mg TDS; child 20 mg/kg/day`,`10 days, after tissue course`,`Flatulence`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`2 g IV once daily, with metronidazole`,`2-3 weeks IV, total 4-6 weeks`,`Allergy, C. difficile`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`Adjust to culture`,`Allergy, low potassium`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral TDS`,`To complete 4-6 weeks`,`Diarrhoea, cholestatic hepatitis`]]},{heading:`Amoebic versus pyogenic liver abscess`,columns:[`Feature`,`Amoebic`,`Pyogenic`],rows:[[`Patient`,`Young man, alcohol`,`Older, diabetic, biliary disease`],[`Number and site`,`Single, right lobe`,`Often multiple, either lobe`],[`Onset`,`Subacute, 1-2 weeks`,`Acute with rigors`],[`Pus`,`Sterile anchovy sauce`,`Foul, culture positive`],[`Key test`,`Amoebic IgG over 90% positive`,`Blood and pus culture, CT`],[`Treatment`,`Metronidazole then luminal agent`,`Drainage, 4-6 weeks antibiotics, source control`]]},{heading:`When to drain an amoebic abscess`,columns:[`Indication`,`Why`],rows:[[`No response after 72-96 hours`,`Drug failure or secondary infection`],[`Size over 5-10 cm or thin liver rim`,`High rupture risk`],[`Left-lobe abscess`,`Risk of pericardial rupture`],[`Impending rupture`,`Prevent pleural or peritoneal spread`],[`Uncertain diagnosis`,`Aspirate for culture`]]}],remember:[`Fever, tender liver and **intercostal tenderness** in a young drinker is amoebic until proved otherwise.`,`Anchovy-sauce pus is **sterile**; amoebae live in the wall.`,`Negative amoebic serology helps more than a positive one.`,`Metronidazole **then a luminal agent**; never metronidazole alone.`,`Drain if no response in **72-96 hours**, over 5-10 cm, or left lobe.`,`Pyogenic: **drain, 4-6 weeks of antibiotics, fix the source**.`,`Pleural spread is commonest; **pericardial rupture is deadliest**.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - amoebic and pyogenic liver abscess`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Localised collection of necrotic material or pus in the liver; **amoebic (commonest in India) vs pyogenic** decides drugs alone vs drainage.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Amoebic** - single, posterosuperior right lobe, sterile anchovy-sauce pus; **pyogenic** - biliary (commonest, multiple), portal, arterial, direct, cryptogenic.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`ALA - **E. histolytica, men 20-50, alcohol (toddy, country liquor)**; PLA - **E. coli, Klebsiella (hypervirulent K1/K2 in diabetics)**, Strep milleri, anaerobes; biliary stones, stents, malignancy, appendicitis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Cysts (resist chlorination) -> colonic trophozoites (**Gal/GalNAc lectin, amoebapores**) -> **portal vein -> right lobe** -> liquefactive necrosis; trophozoites only in the wall; capsule stretch -> RUQ pain, diaphragm -> shoulder tip.`],[`Key history`,`Fever, **RUQ pain to right shoulder worse on breathing**, subacute 1-2 weeks (ALA) vs rigors (PLA), alcohol, diabetes, biliary disease, cough/anchovy sputum, chest pain (left lobe), visual symptoms (Klebsiella).`],[`Key examination`,`**Tender hepatomegaly**, **intercostal tenderness**, right basal dullness, jaundice (biliary PLA), epigastric mass (left lobe), pericardial rub, peritonism, fundi in diabetics.`],[`Investigations`,`CBC (leucocytosis), **raised ALP**, glucose, **USG - hypoechoic lesion abutting capsule**, CXR (**raised right hemidiaphragm**), **amoebic IgG over 90% sensitive**, blood cultures in PLA, CT for complicated/multiple, hydatid serology before aspirating septate cyst.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Clinical + USG lesion + **amoebic serology/antigen/PCR** (ALA) or **positive blood/aspirate culture** (PLA); negative amoebic serology argues strongly against ALA.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`ALA - **metronidazole 750 mg TDS x 7-10 days** (500 mg IV 8-hourly if toxic; child 35-50 mg/kg/day) or **tinidazole 2 g OD x 3-5 days**, then **paromomycin 25-35 mg/kg/day x 7 days or diloxanide 500 mg TDS x 10 days**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Uncertain type - **ceftriaxone 2 g OD + metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly**; ESBL risk - **pip-taz 4.5 g or meropenem 1 g**; PLA **4-6 weeks total (2-3 IV)**, oral amoxiclav or cipro + metronidazole; **source control (ERCP)**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Drain ALA if **no response in 72-96 h, over 5-10 cm, left lobe, impending rupture, secondary infection**; **needle aspiration under 5 cm, catheter drainage larger/PLA**; surgery for rupture with peritonitis or failed drainage; chest drain for empyema.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy - metronidazole after first trimester (or earlier if benefit outweighs); severe hepatic impairment - reduce metronidazole; **diabetic Klebsiella - eye check, intravitreal antibiotics** for endophthalmitis; no alcohol 72 h after metronidazole.`],[`Complications`,`**Pleuropulmonary (commonest)** - effusion, empyema, hepatobronchial fistula; peritoneal rupture; **pericardial rupture from left lobe (deadliest)**; septic shock, metastatic Klebsiella, IVC thrombosis.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sudden abdominal pain with shock, breathlessness/anchovy sputum, left-lobe abscess with chest pain or raised JVP, rigors with jaundice, no improvement at 72-96 h, abscess over 5-10 cm, visual symptoms, septic shock.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review at **72 h**, then weekly; **do not treat the ultrasound** - cavity resolves in 3-12 months; confirm luminal course; **colonoscopy after Klebsiella/Strep milleri abscess**; control diabetes.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Boil or filter water** (cysts resist chlorination), handwashing, sanitation, treat cyst passers and food handlers with luminal agent, notify clusters via **IDSP-IHIP**.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed in this pass - management by Harrison 22e, Sabiston 22e and ICMR STW 2022 remains current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Young alcohol-using man + fever + tender liver + intercostal tenderness = ALA; **anchovy-sauce pus is sterile**; never stop at metronidazole alone; pyogenic needs **drainage + 4-6 weeks + source control**; pericardial rupture deadliest.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Liver abscess: diagnose and decide drainage`,steps:[{label:`Fever + RUQ pain + tender liver`,detail:`Intercostal tenderness, raised right hemidiaphragm`},{label:`USG + amoebic serology + blood cultures`,detail:`Glucose, LFT (ALP), CBC`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Unclear type or septic`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g OD + metronidazole 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Amoebic: metronidazole 750 mg TDS 7-10 d`,detail:`Expect response in 72-96 h`},{label:`No response, over 5-10 cm, left lobe, rupture risk`,detail:`Aspirate (under 5 cm) or catheter drainage`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Luminal agent after the course`,detail:`Paromomycin 7 days or diloxanide 10 days`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Amoebic vs pyogenic liver abscess`,columns:[`Feature`,`Amoebic`,`Pyogenic`],rows:[[`Patient`,`Young man, alcohol`,`Older, diabetic, biliary disease`],[`Number`,`Single, right lobe`,`Often multiple (biliary)`],[`Pus`,`Sterile anchovy sauce`,`Foul, culture positive`],[`Key test`,`Amoebic IgG, antigen/PCR`,`Blood and aspirate culture, CT for source`],[`Treatment`,`Metronidazole then luminal agent`,`Drainage + 4-6 weeks antibiotics + source control`]]}]}};export{e as default};